                                                              Linux Device Driver 3rd Edition
/**
 * Kernel Version used for the Book => 2.6.10
 * Kernel Version used for this note => 2.6.34    
 */

Char Devices :
        scull => Simple Character Utility for Loading Localities
        /* a char driver acts on a memory area as though it were a device */
          [scull0, scull3] => resembling to a shared memory area,
                              global and persistent(re-open no data lost)
          [scullpipe0, scullpipe3] => pipe(several reader happens race cond)
          scullsingle => only allows one process at a time to use
          scullpriv => private to each virtual console
          sculluid, scullwuid => limited to one user at a time,
                                 can be opened multiple times

        Major number
          identifies the driver associated with the device.
          /* modern linux kernel supports major number is shared between
           * several different drivers.
           */
        Minor number
          used by kernel to determine exactly which device is being referred to.

        dev_t
          holds both major number and minor number.
          major number => 12 bits
          minor number => 20 bits
          /* we extract these parts or construct new value through kernel macros.
           * in header <linux/kdev_t.h>
           * in earlier kernel,both numbers are limited to 255 numbers,
           * but recent kernel supports wider range.
           */

        register_chrdev_region
          routine to register char device number region.
          its first parameter is a device number(contains major and minor)
          identifies the start point.

        alloc_chrdev_region
          dynamically allocate char device number region.

        unregister_chrdev_region
          free char device number region.

        ! best to get device number dynamically rather than pick a unused static
          device number.

        most of the fundamental driver operations involve three important kernel
        data structures :
          1> struct file_operations
               device number <=>  driver's operations
                              |
                              +--> file_operations
             NULL operations :
               readdir() <= fs only
               sendfile() <= usually NULL
               sendpage() <= usually NULL
               get_unmapped_area() <= usually NULL
               dir_notify() <= fs only
             The others has default behavior supported by kernel,when design
             character device driver,we need to see what operations the data
             stream need to supports.
             /* usually,lseek() is not supported by data stream like pipe and
              * network socket.
              * if the device is a keyboard,lseek also is not possible.
              */
          2> struct file
             for device file,because "everything is a file".
          3> struct inode
             for filesystem where this device file residing on has an inode
             associated with.
             /* character device driver usually focus on @i_rdev and @i_cdev
              * members.
              * <linux/fs.h>
              *   imajor(*inode) -> major device number
              *   iminor(*inode) -> minor device number
              * these two routines get results from @inode->i_rdev through
              * device number macros.
              * we should prefer to these routines rather one use @i_rdev
              * directly.
              */

        struct cdev
          character device descriptor in kernel space.
    
          allocating and initializing
            cdev_alloc()
          only initializing
            cdev_init()
          add to @chrdevs
            cdev_add() /* no kobject_add() invoked,driver takes charge of it */
          remove from @chrdevs and deallocate
            cdev_del()
          query
            cdev_index() /* use inode query cdev in Kobject Mapping Domain */
    
          ! the @count parameter for cdev_add() is used to tell kernel the
            number of device numbers that should be associated with the
            device.(range of minor numbers)
          ! if we register char device through register_chrdev() path,
            we do not need to invoke cdev_alloc() and cdev_add() manually.
          ! register_chrdev() default will register 0-255 minor numbers for
            the char device,and a cdev descriptor corresponds to these
            device numbers.
            if we want to control the range of minor numbers,we can register
            char device through interfaces cdev_add() or register_chrdev_region().

        open() and release()
          open()
            provided for a driver to do any initialization in preparation for
            later operations.
            /* tasks usually performed :
             *   1> check device-specific errors(such device not ready error)
             *   2> initialize the device if it is being opened for the first
             *      time
             *   3> update @f_op of file descriptor if necessary
             *   4> allocate and fill any data structure to be put in
             *      @private_data of file descriptor
             */
            the device file inode is will be passed as the first parameter of
            open() method.(in which,@i_cdev is the char device descriptor)
            /* if we wrapped cdev in custom device descriptor,the macro
             * container_of() can helps us to extract that descriptor.
             */

          release()
            reverse of open().
            /* tasks need to perform :
             *   1> deallocate anything that open allocated in @private_data of
             *      file descriptor
             *   2> shut down the device on last close
             */
            ! flush() method usually is when close() is called,but very
              few drivers implement flush() method.

        read() and write()
          read()
            copy data from device to userspace,usually depends on copy_to_user().
            if there is no more data available,we need to return EOF or block
            current task until new data has came in.
            /* for network stream,we usually need to wait for new data come in */
            /**
             * return value :
             *   == @count => transferred all
             *   > 0 AND < @count => a part
             *   == 0 => EOF
             *   < 0 => error
             * in userspace,EOF is defined to "-1".
             */

          write()
            data is written out from userspace to device,usually depends on
            copy_from_user().
            generally,write operation is less blocking.if device is not ready
            to receives user's data,we need to return error or wait for device
            become ready.
            /**
             * return value :
             *   == @count => transferred all
             *   > 0 AND < @count => a part
             *                       # usually happens in network transferring
             *   == 0 => failure reason specific to device(not error)
             *   < 0 => error
             */

          ! if data size from userspace or to userspace is greater than PAGE_SIZE,
            kernel would not guarantees atomic operation(copy_to_user() and
            copy_from_user()).
            the two helper also would check whether userspace pointer is valid,
            no copying will performed otherwise.
            /* if an invalid address is encountered during the copy,only part of
             * the data is copied.
             * ! but in linux 2.6.34.1,for the x86 platform,access_ok() is used
             *   to check userspace pointer,if invalid address is found,no
             *   data would be copied,even a part of the data.
             * for the inatomic copying,if a memory area of userspace eventually
             * becomed invalid,only a part of data is copied.
             */

          ! read() and write() returns the size of transferred data,even only a
            part of data is transffered for some special reasons,no error will
            be reported at this time if it occurred.
            but at the next time these methods are called,the previous error
            would be reported,so our driver needs to remember the error has
            occurred so that it can return the error status in the future.
            /* specific to device driver operations */
              struct scull_dev *device = (struct scull_dev *)flip->private_data;
              if (device->last_error)
                      return last_error;
              ...
              ret = device_read(...);
              if (ret && ret < count) {
                      status = device_status_query(...);
                      if (status > 0)
                              device->last_error = -EIO;
              } else if (ret == count) {
                      device->last_error = 0;
                      ...
              }
              ...

        readv() and writev()
          these two system calls are supported by kernel,and they rely on
          driver's read and write methods,respectively.
          in this case,these vector versions will be implemented with
          multiple calls to read and write methods of our driver.
          /**
           * in the early version of kernel,file_operations struct contains
           * readv() and writev() methods,but in kernel 2.6.34.1,they are been
           * removed from file_operations,and the readv(),writev() entrypoints
           * are vfs_readv() and vfs_writev(),respectively.
           * if file operations defined aio_read(),then readv() relies on it,
           * otherwise fallback to a loop of read() method;the writev() relies
           * on aio_write(),fallback to a loop of writev() if such function is
           * not implemented.
           */

Advanced Char Driver Operations :
        ioctl        
          this interface usually is offered for hardware device control.
          for example,lock its door,report error,change baud rate,etc.
          if we want our device to provides such interface,we must implement
          the ioctl() in file_operations struct.
          /**
           * Kernel provides some static ioctl commands that been integered into
           * kernel.
           * if we want our ioctl CMDs also enter the kernel,we need put them
           * into some related kernel file,and re-compile kernel,this is not
           * a good idea.
           * so we can provides these CMD definitions into userspace,via a
           * header comes with out driver module.
           * user can knows what commands our driver provides through check
           * the header.
           */
          ! "..." in ioctl() prototype in userspace library is not means it
            receives variable number of parameters,it just receives an
            additional optional parameter.
            traditionally,the last parameter is identified as "char *argp".
            # just to prevent type checking during compilation.
            and because,the type checking is disabled,the bad third parameter
            to ioctl() will cause undefined behavior without compiler warning.
          each CMD of kernel ioctl(),essentially is a separate,usually
          undocumented system call,there is no way to audit these calls in
          any sort of comprehensive manner.

        ioctl commands selection
          the entrypoint of ioctl facility is do_vfs_ioctl(),which is defined in
          <fs/ioctl.c> and called by sys_ioctl().
          in that routine,only some common file-related ioctl commands are
          defined.
          for the commands that are not matches to those commands,the default
          path will jump to vfs_ioctl() or to file_ioctl(),if the file is
          regular file,then file_ioctl(),otherwise vfs_ioctl().
          in vfs_ioctl(),unlocked_ioctl() of file_operations struct is prefered
          than ioctl() of file_operations struct.
          the difference between them is that,ioctl() is locked version,
          vfs_ioctl() calls to this function need to invoke lock_kernel() to
          lock kernel at first,and unlock kernel later after operation finished.
          for unlocked_ioctl() branch,-ENOIOCTLCMD will be checked and convert
          to -EINVAL.
          thus,we must ensure that our ioctl commands' numbers are not in the
          common ioctl commands that kernel defined.
          the list of common commands :
            FIOCLEX                set close on exec
            FIONCLEX               unset close on exec
            FIONBIO                no blocking io(O_NONBLOCK flag)
            FIOASYNC               async io
            FIOQSIZE               get file size through inode_get_bytes()
            FIFREEZE               filesystem freeze
            FITHAW                 filesystem thaw
            FS_IOC_FILEMAP         file map
            FIGETBSZ               get block size of the super block that
                                   current inode belongs to
            ! those pre-defined commands can be classified to three groups
                for any files(include FIFO,socket, ...)
                for regular files only
                specific to filesystem(depends on filesystem implementation,
                                       for example,"chattr" command)
            ! except the list,more global kernel ioctl command numbers can be
              found in <Documentation/ioctl-number.txt>,those ioctl command
              numbers been used by some kernel drivers,we should prevent
              reuse them in our driver.
              # newer kernel is
                  <Documentation/userspace-api/ioctl/ioctl-number.rst>
                in that file, "Code" means type,"Seq" means numbers.
            ! if we just for test,our driver code can reuse those numbers,
              but if we are developing a driver for real device in real world,
              and want it will be received by kernel,we must choose the free
              ioctl command numbers.
              #^ if our ioctl() interface will uses some kernel routines
                 with our ioctl command number,must prevent conflict with those
                 in-use numbers.
                 actually,vfs_ioctl() will invoke our device file's ioctl
                 interface,reuse the same number is no problem,but if our ioctl
                 interface will across kernel routines,reuse same number will
                 cause undefined behavior probably.
                 we prevent from reuse at the starting time can help us to
                 get rid of such kind of problems,because we usually
                 forget some important things.
    
          ! the older kernel use 16 bits for ioctl command,but the newer kernel
            changed to use 32 bits for ioctl command.
            in lower 16 bits of 32 bits,top 8 bits for type,and bottom 8 bits
            for number.

        ioctl return value
          -ENOTTY => inappropriate ioctl command(POSIX standard)
          -EINVAL => invalid ioctl command
          -ENOIOCTLCMD => appears in kernel ioctl code,no such ioctl command

        Capabilities and Restricted Operations
          driver must performs additional capabilities checking to determines
          whether current user is granted to executes particular operations
          on the device.
          the User Mode system calls capget() and capset() can be used to
          get and set a thread's capabilities respectively.
          ! if we want to add a new capability,need to modify the kernel source.
          some device driver related capabilities
            CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE        override access restrictions on files and
                                    directories
                                    DAC => data access control
            CAP_NET_ADMIN           ability to perform network adiministration
                                    tasks
            CAP_SYS_MODULE          load or remove kernel modules
            CAP_SYS_RAWIO           ability to perform 'raw' I/O operations
                                    such access device ports and communicate
                                    directly with USB devices
            CAP_SYS_ADMIN           for system administration operations
            CAP_SYS_TTY_CONFIG      ability to perform tty configuration tasks
          ! we usually return -EPERM if current thread has no requested
            capability.

        Control Device via Control Sequence
          we can configure a device by write control sequence to the device
          itself,rather than issue an ioctl system call.
          this way requires the device driver can responses to such control
          sequence.
          /* for example,"setterm" command for console can be used to send
           * console control sequence.
           * ! controlling program can live on a different computer from
           *   the controlled device,a simple redirection of the data stream
           *   does the configuration job.
           * # the sysfs or /proc/ for kernel special options setup is similar
           *   to write control sequence to device.
           */
          drawback
            controlling by printing is that it adds policy constraints to
            the device.
            we need to ensure the control sequence can not appear in the
            data being written to the device during normal operation.
            /* when we "cat" a binary file,the result is console's font
             * became weird.
             */
          instead of turning the "write" method into an interpreter and
          avoiding "ioctl",we can avoid "write" altogether and use "ioctl
          commands" exclusively.that is,provide a command-line tool to
          send those commands to the driver,this can moves the complexity
          from kernel to userspace.

        Blocking I/O
          blocking I/O means we need to put the task into sleep state or
          add it into a wait queue because operations cant be accomplished
          right now.
          if we decide to put it into waiting state,must invoke the kernel
          function schedule() to give up CPU time let the scheduler to
          selects next task to run.
          blocking rules
            > cant sleep in atomic operation
            > cant sleep in interrupt context
            > cant sleep when holding a lock
            > cant sleep when local interrupt been disabled
            > cant sleep if there is no one will wake up this task        
            > can sleep when holding a semaphore,but this sleep must be
              short because other tasks may also sleep to wait this
              semaphore
            > need to re-check neccessary conditions after been waken up
          some kernel sleep related primatives
            /**
             * these routines will add our task into wait queue,and
             * call to schedule().
             * the loop will be broken when the condition becomes true.
             * waiting tasks are grouped into non-exclusive tasks and
             * exclusive tasks.
             * the wake_up will always wakes up all non-exclusive tasks,
             * and some exclusive tasks(one or more,or all -- number of
             * exclusive tasks to be woken up is 0 <= non-exclusive wake up).
             * kernel implements this by put exclusive tasks at the
             * tail of wait queue,when we traverse the list,all
             * non-exclusive tasks will always be woken up.
             */
            wait_event(wq, cond)
            wait_event_interruptible
            wait_event_timeout
            wait_event_interruptible_timeout

            wake_up(wq)
            wake_up_interruptible

            wait_for_completion(completion)
            wait_for_completion_killable
            wait_for_completion_interruptible
            wait_for_completion_timeout
    
            complete(completion)
            complete_all

          !O_NONBLOCK driver behavior
            "read" blocking if no data available,and woken up as soon as the
            data arrives,then the data is returned to userspace process even
            the size of data is less than @count.

            "write" blocking if no space in data buffer,and it must be on a
            different wait queue from the one used for reading.
            the task is woken up as soon as it can write data,and "write"
            system call succeeds,even there is only a part been written.
                                 #^ no room for all data count by @count

            /**
             * driver provides input buffer and output buffer can reduce number
             * of context switches and user-level/kernel-level transitions.
             * because driver can accepts a large chunk of data,and it can buffer
             * the data from userspace,transfer those data to the device when the
             * size reach the defined threshold.
             * (the example is BIO in kernel for block device I/O)
             */

          O_NONBLOCK driver behavior
            just returns -EAGAIN if request cant be satisified now.

          ! drivers may provide O_NONBLOCK open() method and !O_NONBLOCK open()
            method.(deal with two cases in the implementation)
            because,some devices need a long time to initialization.for the open
            without blocking,-EAGAIN should be returns immediately if the device
            is initializing;for the open with blocking,the task should wait for
            the device fully initialized.
            /* blocking open as an alternative to EBUSY */

          ! we should not use sleep_on() and its variants in driver code,because
            it offers no way to protect against race conditions.
            it is implemented through construct a wait_queue_t object and adds
            the object into wait_queue_head_t the wait queue,then invoke
            schedule_timeout() with maximum timeout.
            if a wakeup come right before schedule() is called,and there is
            only once wakeup,we miss it and sleep forever.
            # actually,kernel code should not sleep unconditionally.

        poll and select
          query and wait for open files are readable or writeable without
          blocking.
          if we want our device driver support this I/O Multiplexing,we must
          provide the poll() method implementation of file_operations struct
          of our driver.
          ! do_select() also depends on poll components.
    
          system call service routine will call to our custom poll() method
          when user issued poll() or select() system call.
          system call service routine sys_poll() hands over the main works
          to do_sys_poll() function.which function will creates a local
          poll_wqueues object,initialize it,and pass it to do_poll().
          /**
           * poll_wqueues
           *   ->pt             : poll_table
           *   ->table          : struct poll_table_page *
           *   ->polling_task   : struct task_struct *
           *   ->triggered      : int
           *   ->error          : int
           *   ->inline_index   : int
           *   ->inline_entries : struct poll_table_entry[]
           *                      N_INLINE_POLL_ENTRIES
           * # if there is a room in @inline_entries,no poll_table_page
           *   would be newly allocated.
           */
          do_poll() will walk-through a poll_list,for each pollfd object
          on the list,it iterates each fd that contained in the range
          defined by this pollfd object,call do_pollfd() on those fds.
          /**
           * poll_list
           *   ->next    : struct poll_list *
           *   ->len     : int
           *   ->entries : struct pollfd[0]
           * # @next    => next poll_list entry on list
           *   @len     => number of elements in @entries
           *   @entries => copied from userspace
           * # new poll_list object is allocated via kmalloc() with gfp flag
           *   GFP_KERNEL
           */
          the do_pollfd(),will invokes the poll() method of the file
          descriptor that corresponding to current fd(if the method is
          implemented).
          ! if timeout value is set to 0,then enter no-wait case,do_poll() will
            set @pt to NULL.hence,poll_wait() will returns immediately without
            adds any wait entity into driver's poll wait queue.
            after a loop for calling to do_pollfd(),do_poll() will ends the
            external loop without calls to poll_schedule_timeout().
            # timeout value -1 means wait infinitely.
            # do_pollfd() still calls to driver's poll() method,but the
              poll_wait() called by driver code has no effect,so poll()
              of driver should returns the result mask immediately.
          ! the pollfd array is copied from userspace into poll_list's
            @entries member,and the member @len means the amount of
            pollfd(s) in that array.
          ! poll_list structure is defined in <fs/select.c>,and
            poll_table structure is defined in <linux/poll.h>.
            the poll_table pointer passed to poll() file operation
            points to the poll_wqueues->pt member.
            /**
             * poll_table_struct 
             *   ->qproc : poll_queue_proc
             *   ->key   : unsigned long
             * (type-definition poll_table)
             */
            member @pt is initialized by init_poll_functpr(),which
            function will sets the @qproc function pointer of @pt
            to __pollwait().
            /**
             * type of @qproc
             *   typedef void (*poll_queue_proc)(struct file *,
             *                                   wait_queue_head_t *,
             *                                   struct poll_table_struct *);
             */
            __pollwait() is used to adds a new poll wait entry
            into the poll_table_page of poll_wqueues.
            /**
             * poll_table_entry
             *   ->filp         : struct file *
             *   ->key          : unsigned long
             *   ->wait         : wait_queue_t
             *   ->wait_address : wait_queue_head_t *
             * # @wait_address => usually points to the driver's poll wait
             *                    queue
             *   @filp         => the file
             *   @key          => assigned from @pt->key
             *   @wait         => newly initialized by __pollwait(),and
             *                    will be added into @wait_address,the
             *                    wake-up function is pollwake()
             *
             * poll_table_page
             *   ->next    : struct poll_table_page *
             *   ->entry   : struct poll_table_entry *
             *   ->entries : struct poll_table_entry[0]
             * # defined in <fs/select.c>.
             * # @next    => next poll_table_page struct on the list
             *   @entry   => usually points to @entries
             *   @entries => array of poll_table_entry(s)
             * # routine poll_get_entry() is used to get next poll table entry
             *   from poll table page.this function also takes care of
             *   allocation of new poll table page.
             *   new page is allocated through __get_free_page(GFP_KERNEL),
             *   the starting region of new page contains poll_table_page struct.
             *   new page is inserted front to the old list of poll_wqueues.
             *   the first entry slot of a new poll table page start from
             *   index "1" of @entries.
             *     new_table->entry = new_table->entries;
             *     new_table->next = table;
             *     p->table = new_table;
             *     table = new_table;
             *     return table->entry++;
             *   this is because,at the time free poll waiters,@entry and
             *   @entries will be combined as stop condition of loop
             *     @p->entry > @p->entries
             *     then
             *       free entry
             *       --@p->entry
             */
            its prototype is
              static void __pollwait(struct file *filp,
                                     wait_queue_head_t *wait_address,
                                     poll_table *p);
            and defined in <fs/select.c>.
            inline function poll_wait() can be called by our driver
            to invoke @qproc of @pt to adds a new entry.
              poll_wait(filp, &dev->inq, pt);
              # 1st param => file descriptor
                2nd param => wait_queue_head_t *
                3rd param => poll_table
                             #^ @pt passed by do_pollfd()
          ! unsigned int poll(*@filp, *@poll_table)
            # will returns a mask indicates the I/O state of the
              @filp,the mask is constructed from poll event bits.
            # the poll_table @pt got from the poll_wqueues object.

          the steps that driver's poll() method need to does
            1> invoke poll_wait() on one or more wait queues that could
               indicate a change in the poll status.if no file descriptors
               are currently available for I/O,the kernel causes the process
               to wait on the wait queues for all file descriptors passed
               to the system call.
            2> return a bit mask describing the operations(if any) that
               could be immediately performed without blocking.

          available poll status
            POLLIN                readable without blocking
            POLLRDNORM            "normal" data is available for reading
            POLLRDBAND            "out-of-band" data is available for reading
            POLLPRI               high-priority data(out-of-band) can be read
                                  without blocking
            POLLHUP               EOF
            POLLERR               poll error,readable and writeable,read and
                                  write return an error code without blocking
            POLLOUT               writeable without blocking
            POLLWRNORM            "normal" writeable
            POLLWRBAND            "out-of-band" writeable

            ! for a device is readable,state POLLIN | POLLRDNORM should be
              returned by poll() method.

          the poll_wait() called by our driver will put the device file's
          @filp into the poll_table_entry,if all pollfd(s) are not ready,
          then kernel will invoke poll_schedule_timeout() to let current
          sleep until some event is happened,the wakeup function is
          __pollwake(),the wrapper is pollwake() function,and it will be
          set to the wake-up function of the wait entry that is constructed
          in __pollwait().
          if some state have changed,our driver's component should invoke the
          wake_up() for poll waiters that are waiting on the wait queue is
          passed to poll_wait() previously.
          ! the resume is not from poll_wait() or driver's poll() method,
            because in which no schedule() function is called.
            the resume point is the function do_poll(),and the loop for
            checking filp(s) state is restart again,do_pollfd() is called
            on those filp(s).
            # pollwake() will invoke default_wake_function() on a dummy wait
              queue,in which the task is the member polling_task of that
              poll_wqueues object which @pt belongs to.
              (we just need it to wakeup the task that is blocking on
               sys_poll() now)
            # the removing to poll waiter's wait_queue_t objects is deferred
              to the time that do_poll() returned,in routine poll_freewait().
              (signal interrupt also cause do_poll() returns,with error code
               -EINTR)
            # there is only once add_wait_queue() is called by __pollwait(),
              because after all poll waiters been registered,do_poll() will
              set pointer @pt to NULL,then poll_wait() called by driver's
              poll() method will just returns with do nothing.
              /* if do_pollfd() returns a non-zero value -- state changed,
               * local counter @count will be increased by do_poll(),and it
               * will set @pt to NULL,then next calling to do_pollfd() in
               * the internal loop will not add any poll wait entity to the
               * driver's poll wait queue.(because @pt == NULL)
               * this is because,if any file's state is changed,the current
               * task should not sleep,thus call to poll_wait() with a
               * non-NULL @pt is nosense.(do not need to consume CPU time
               * to build wait list)
               */
            e.g.
              pipe_poll:
                ...
                poll_wait(filp, &des->inq, pt);
                poll_wait(filp, &des->outq, pt);    
                ...
                if (!des->input_buffer.empty)
                  return POLLIN | POLLRDNORM;
                if (des->output_buffer.empty)
                  return POLLOUT | POLLWRNORM;
                ...
              pipe_write:
                ...
                /* set des->input_buffer.empty to FALSE */
                fill_datum_entry(...);
                /**
                 * wake up the poll waiter(s) on input queue
                 */
                wake_up(des->inq);
                ...

              pipe_read:
                ...
                /* set des->output_buffer.empty to TRUE */
                remove_datum_entry(...);
                /**
                 * wake up the poll waiter(s) on output queue
                 */
                wake_up(des->outq);
                ...

          execution flow
            application -> sys_poll() -> do_sys_poll() -> do_poll()
            do_poll() :
              loop0 {
                for each node on poll_list
                  for each fd in node's entries
                    call to do_pollfd()
                      do_pollfd() :
                        f_op->poll(@filp, @pt) :
                          poll_wait(@filp, @wait_address, @pt) :
                            if @pt == NULL || @wait_address
                              return
                    if result is not _zero_ /* state changed,even error occurs */
                    then
                      @count++
                      @pt = NULL /* at least one file's state changed */
                @pt = NULL /* set to NULL to prevent re-add in next iteration */
                if !@count
                  @count = signal_pending(current) ? -EINTR : wait->error
                if @count || @timed_out
                  break
                poll_schedule_timeout()
              }

        reading data from the device
          > data in input buffer
              read returns immediately(no noticeable delay)
              # regardless amount of data less than requested
              # poll returns POLLIN | POLLRDNORM
          > no data in input buffer
              O_NONBLOCK
                read returns immediately with error code -EAGAIN
                # poll returns POLLERR
              !O_NONBLOCK
                read blocks current task until at least one byte of data
                arrived
                # poll suspend current task
          > EOF
              read returns immediately with return value _zero_
              # regardless to O_NONBLOCK
              # poll returns POLLHUP

          ! those rules can be bent for special cases.

        writing to the device
          > has space in output buffer
              write returns immediately without delay
              # even the room is not enough to holds requested size of data
                (at least,one byte)
              # poll returns POLLOUT | POLLWRNORM
          > has no space in output buffer
              O_NONBLOCK
                write returns immediately with error code -EAGAIN
                # poll returns POLLERR
              !O_NONBLOCK
                write blocks current task until there is a space to holds
                data,at least one byte

              # whether or not O_NONBLOCK is setting,if device is not ready
                to accept data,-ENOSPC should be returned
          > never make a write call wait for data transmission before returning,
            even if O_NONBLOCK is clear
            # from kernel buffer(allocated by driver) to the real device
            # this is because if poll() or select() reported writeable,the write
              must not block.
              if program wants to ensure the data issued actually transmitted,
              driver needs to provide an fsync() method.

          ! those rules can be bent for special cases.    

        fsync method
          driver can provides this method to let the program can determine the
          data in output buffer actually sent to the real device.
          # regardless of whether or not O_NONBLOCK is set
          fsync() returns when the data all have been transmitted,otherwise it
          needs to block current task to wait complement.
          this method's third parameter is @datasync,it is used to tell kernel
          do data sync only(== 1) or include metadata(== 0).
          filesystem needs to handle metadata,but device driver does not.

          /**
           * no special requirements on the implementation of this method.
           * only ensure when the function returns,the data must been
           * transmitted.
           * char device driver can leaves this function pointer to NULL.
           * block device driver can make use of fsync_bdev() which is defined
           * in <fs/block_dev.c>.
           */

        asynchronous notification
          signal is asynchronous,so we can use it to implement asynchronous
          notification from kernel to user process.
          when a user process wants to receives asynchronous signal on a given
          file,it must invoke fcntl with F_SETOWN on the file descriptor.this
          will let kernel saves the process id into @f_owner of the file's
          descriptor.
          ! the user process also need to set FASYNC flag via fcntl() with
            F_SETFL.
            # we can activate this when opening file set O_ASYNC flag for
              open().
          ! asynchronous I/O notification is useful only for there is only
            one file setup asynchronous I/O notification.because the signals
            delivered by kernel thread does not carry any additional information
            except the signal number -- SIGIO.hence,if there is more than one
            file setup asynchronous I/O notification,we can not distinguish
            which one corresponds to current signal.

          /**
           * when we invoke F_SETFL with FASYNC,the kernel will invoke the
           * fasync() method of the file's operations,if such method
           * is implemented.
           * kernel provides fasync_helper(),it will creates and adds a new
           * fasync entry for current file,and set FASYNC flag.
           * if we have no special requirements on our driver,we can make use
           * of this helper like the pipe component did.
           * the 3rd parameter of fasync() is a boolean value @on.
           * if @on is not true,fasync_helper() needs to remove fasync entry of
           * current file.
           * ! the entries are reside on list has type struct fasync_struct.
           *   fasync() method should passes such list to fasync_helper().
           *   # we pass the address of the list descriptor.
           *     => struct fasync_struct **
           */

        driver behavior for FASYNC
          > driver's descriptor needs to hold a fasync list -- the type is
            struct fasync_struct *.
            /**
             * <linux/fs.h>
             *   fasync_struct
             *     ->magic   : int
             *     ->fa_fd   : int
             *     ->fa_next : struct fasync_struct *
             *     ->fa_file : struct file *
             *   # @magic   => fasync magic number,it is 0x4601(FASYNC_MAGIC)
             *     @fa_fd   => file descriptor in userspace
             *     @fa_next => next entry
             *     @fa_file => open file descriptor in kernel space
             *   # fasync_helper() will creates such entry and insert it into
             *     fasync list.
             */
          > driver needs to implement fasync() method,which usually relies on
            fasync_helper().
            the last argument to the function is fasync list for current device,
            and often has two lists to corresponds two directions.(in / out)
            for read operations,should pass read fasync list.
            for write operations,should pass write fasync list.
          > if I/O event happens,invoke kill_fasync() to signal file's owner.
            /**
             * <fs/fcntl.c>
             *   # kill_fasync - signal waiters on fasync list
             *   # @fp:          fasync list
             *   # @sig:         signal will delivered to f_owner
             *   # @band:        data type,band data or out-of-band data
             *                   for readable,usually is POLL_IN
             *                   for writeable,usually is POLL_OUT
             *                   (POLL_IN == POLLIN | POLLRDNORM
             *                    POLL_OUT == POLLOUT | POLLWRNORM)
             *   void kill_fasync(struct fasync_struct **fp, int sig, int band);
             *   # EXPORT_SYMBOL
             *   # this routine calls to __kill_fasync() with @fasync_lock
             *     holding if *@fp is not NULL
             *   # __kill_fasync(),the primary worker.
             *     traverses @fa,for each entry has FASYNC_MAGIC,invoke
             *     send_sigio() to the file's owner if and only if
             *     @sig is not SIGURG AND f_owner's @signum is not _zero_.
             *     ! for bad fasync magic,a KERN_ERR msg would be printed.
             */
           > when device file is closed,need to invoke fasync() method again
             in the release() method,to remove fasync entry -- pass the 3rd
             parameter _zero_ of fasync() method can let fasync_helper()
             remove entry(@on == 0).
           ! there is no requirement that the device can only has one f_owner,
             because for each open file,a file descriptor is created,and each
             file descriptor are differen from the first one,even they refers
             to the same file.
             (if we create new task with CLONE_FILES,the threads will share the
              same file descriptor)
             thus,it is not hard to imagine there would be a lot of processes
             waiting on the device's fasync list,kill_fasync() will signal to
             each waiting process.
             F_SETFL will adds an entry into the device's fasync list,and
             release() will remove an entry from the device's fasync list.

        seeking device
          for char device,it similar to data stream.
          seek a char device,is likely to adjust file descriptor's @f_pos.
          the new @f_pos is equal to
            base point + relative offset
          the base points are
            SEEK_SET    => starting position
            SEEK_CURR   => current position
            SEEK_END    => ending position
          if our device is a keyboard,it should not supports device seeking.
          if our device is a pipe,it should supports device seeking like a
          regular file's seeking.
          after seeking,the file descriptor's @f_pos should be up to dated.
    
          ! if our device does not support llseek(),we must invoke the routine
            nonseekable_open() in our open() method,to tell kernel such truth.
            nonseekable_open() is defined in <fs/open.c>,which will disable
            flags FMODE_LSEEK | FMODE_PREAD | FMODE_PWRITE for our filp.
            the future calling to llseek() on this file,kernel will ensures
            that would not success,and we also need to let llseek() method
            of our driver points to no_llseek() routine.
            # no_llseek() is defined in <fs/read_write.c>,it simply returns
              -ESPIPE.

          ! llseek on block device is more complex,because that would relate
            to disk addressing.
            the generic method provided by kernel is block_llseek(),which will
            adjust file's @f_pos,when Generic Block Layer issues request to
            disk,the disk controller should moves head to correct position,
            then we can read data from the position we expected.
            (block device has minimum read size -- block size,the real reading
             position may start from the block our expected position falling
             into it)

        Access Control on Device
          Single-Open Devices
            permit a device can be opened by only one process at a time,but this
            method best to be avoided.
            if we only keep a track of a open counter in driver descriptor,any
            future open request from processes of the same user would be refused.
              e.g.
                process A open file <= counter increase
                process A start system() call
                  new shell try to open the same file <= counter is 0,
                                                         resource unavailable
            to implement this access control is very easy,just let the driver
            descriptor holds an atomic integer,initial value is 1,decrease
            when open is permitted,increase in release.

          Single-User Devices
            similar to single-open device,but we rather to hold the user's
            effect ID,and a counter.
            the counter is used to keep track of times the device is opened.
            if saved user ID is not equal to current->uid and current->euid,
            we need to refuse this request;but for root user,it is always
            permitted to open the device.
              e.g.
                spin_lock(des->single_usr_lock);
                if (device_open_counter &&
                    saved_uid != current->uid &&
                    saved_uid != current->euid &&
    
                    /* or use CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE for this test */
                    current->uid != 0) {
    
                        spin_unlock(des->single_usr_lock);
                        return -EBUSY;
                }

                if (!device_open_counter)
                        saved_uid = current->uid;
                ++device_open_counter;
                spin_unlock(des->single_usr_lock);
            we perform these checking in open(),restore the saved id and
            the counter in release() method.
            /* we can just restore open counter to 0,then new user id
             * would be saved in open().
             */

        Blocking open as an Alternative to EBUSY
          the example is pipe,if we try to write open a pipe with blocking,
          process would be blocked until there is another entity read opened
          this pipe.
          if we want to implement this mechanism,we need to use the wait queue,
          and let the driver wakes up waiting processes in the proper driver
          method(event happens).
          if O_NONBLOCK is specified,open() should returns -EAGAIN instead wait
          silently.
            e.g.
              spin_lock(des->status_lock);
              while (query_status(des) != DEV_AVAILABLE) {
                spin_unlock(des->status_lock);
                if (filp->f_flags & O_NONBLOCK)
                        return -EAGAIN;
                if (wait_event_interruptible(
                            des->wq,
                            query_status(dev) == DEV_AVAILABLE))
                        return -ERESTARTSYS;
                spin_lock(des->status_lock);
                /**
                 * query_status() does not acquire @status_lock,
                 * if we was woken up,we need to check the status
                 * again with lock is holding.
                 * we also can design lock version :
                 *   acquire lock before perform test
                 *   return TRUE with lock is holding
                 *   return FALSE with lock is released
                 * ! query_status() is read-side,thus no lock is not a problem
                 *   at there because we will re-check the condition as soon as
                 *   we have woken up.
                 *   if the routine related to write operation,race condition
                 *   must be prevent at the start.
                 *   we often put us into wait queue because we want to wait
                 *   for a state is changed by someone,thus there is rarely
                 *   write operation occurs.
                 */
              }
              ...
              spin_unlock(des->status_lock);

          ! some command line tools would not set O_NONBLOCK in open flags,
            the default is blocking open.if the device is not available now,
            return -EBUSY or -EAGAIN is better than blocking on open() method.
            (interactive user)

        Cloning the Device on Open
          ! this requires the device is not bound to hardware object.
          we can make different copies of the original device for each different
          processes,as private copy.
          virtual devices :
            the copies of the device are created by the software driver.
          (for example,virtual console and physical tty)
          the most easy way to implement this is make use of a list to collects
          all virtual devices.
          open :
            use identify information try to find the device from list,if no such
            device,we create a new virtual device and add it into collection.
            /* actually,userspace file descriptor is no sense at there,because
             * each process has its own private copy.
             */
            e.g.
              int ret = dev_try_find(dev_pid2key(current->pid));
              return ret;

              dev_try_find :
                list_for_each(...) to find entity has the same key.
                if no such key,try to allocate a new virtual device,and
                initialize it with this key.
                allocation failed return -ENOMEM.
                creation success return 0.
          release :
            as usual,release the resource corresponding to the private copy at
            the last close -- because each process has one private copy,thus
            the reference counter usually is 1.
            /* deallocate the virtual device */
          read and write :
            operates only on the virtual device this process owns.if no such
            virtual device,need to return an error.

          ! if the software device is a in-memory device,we can defer the memory
            allocation for device buffer until the first I/O operation is
            issued.
            the release() method,need to release the device buffer and virtual
            device if its reference counter down to _zero_.

Communicating with Hardware :    
        I/O Ports and I/O Memory
          peripheral device is controlled by writing and reading its registers.
          those registers usually is mapped to either the _memory address space_
          or _I/O address space_.

          I/O Memory -
            peripheral device's registers are mapped into the memory address
            space,and the region for those registers is called I/O Memory.
            when the I/O Memory is allocated to a peripheral device,from CPU
            view point,this memory region represents the peripheral device.
            /* the device memory also is included in I/O Memory term */
            it is simply a region of RAM-like locations that the device makes
            available to the processor over the bus.

          I/O Port -
            peripheral device's registers are mapped into a separate address
            space,the address space is different from the memory address space
            that now visible to the CPU.
            the mapping on the separate address space is called I/O Port.

          L
          0x00000200
          [ reg0 | reg1 | ... ] <= peripheral device register mapping
          | |                 0x00000400
          | |                 H
          | +--> an I/O port
          V
          |<----------------->|<----------------->|<----------------->|
                           I/O BUS Memory Address Space
            ^
            |
            |
            peripheral device needs to register it on the proper bus
    
          ! for hardware,the memory region same as the I/O region.
            I/O command through address bus and control bus.
            data transferring through data bus.

          ! some CPU implements a single address space,that peripheral devices
            are different from memory and,deserve a separate address space.
            # I/O ports for peripheral devices are reside on this special
              address space,differen from the memory address space where tasks
              resides on.
              x86 provides such I/O address space.
    
          ! x86 CPU family have separate read and write electrical lines for
            I/O ports and special CPU instructions to access ports.

          ! even if the peripheral bus has a separate address space for
            I/O ports,not all devices map their registers to I/O ports.

          ! architectures that support only memory-mapped I/O registers fake
            port I/O by remapping port address to memory addresses.

        I/O Registers and Conventional Memory
          main difference between I/O registers and RAM :
            I/O operations have side effects,while memory operations have none.
          RAM operation optimizations :
            /* no-side-effects */
            1> value cache
            2> read/write instructions reorder

            ! we must to avoid the I/O register operation is optimized by CPU
              or compiler that they think this is a RAM operation.
              for this reason,the driver needs to ensure no caching is
              performed and no read or write reordering when accesses to
              the device's registers.

          if we want to prevent reordering,we need to put a memory barrier
          between I/O register operation and other RAM operation.
          /* the barrier also can prevent compiler optimization */

          routines that make a memory barrier :
            <linux/compiler-gcc.h>
              barrier()
                just use an inline assembly statement with no instructions,
                but mark memory is changed.
                  asm("":::"memory");
                ! prevent compiler optimizations across the barrier,but leaves
                  the hardware free to do its own reordering.

            <arch/x86/include/asm/system.h>
              /* hardware memory barrier */
              mb()
                mfence
                /* barrier for reads and writes */
              rmb()
                lfence
                /* reads only */
              wmb()
                sfence
                /* writes only */

              /* SMP version */
              smp_mb()
              smp_rmb()
              smp_wmb()

              read_barrier_depends()
                flush all pending reads that subsequents reads depends on.
                /* blocks only the reordering of reads that depend on data
                 * from other reads.
                 */

              /* SMP version */
              smp_read_barrier_depends()

            example :
              writeb(0, device->registers.control);
              writeb(2, device->registers.operation);
              wmb();
              writel(4, device->registers.operation);

          ! use memory barrier where it is really needed.
            kind of operation needs to correspond the barrier's type.

        Using I/O Ports
          struct resource
            I/O port resource tree node.
            used to represents a set of I/O ports.
            instead to stores the I/O port numbers,kernel stores I/O memory
            region address in this structure.
            ! the root of I/O port tree is @ioport_resource,it represents
              I/O port number 0 -- 65535.
    
          allocation
            struct resource *request_region(@start, @n, @name)
            # @start => resource start position(memory address)
                        type is resource_size_t <= phys_addr_t
              @n     => number of memory units
              @name  => name of the resource,for our device
            # request a resource region of this system.
            ! resource is used to represents the I/O memory region,
              but the I/O port is mapped to device register,there is no
              guarantee the size of port always 8 bits,its size usually
              is depends on size of device's register.

          release
            void release_region(@start, @n)

          query
            int check_region(@start, @n)
            /* __deprecated */
            /* 0 => region is free now
             * -EBUSY => region is busy
             * ! checking and allocation is not an atomic operation,
             *   thus no guarantee the allocation would be success
             *   later.
             *   we should not use this routine,instead,we try to
             *   request region directly through request_region(),
             *   its result tells us whether the region is available.
             */

          manipulating I/O ports
            inb                  read I/O port and return 8bits
            readb                read iomem and return 8 bits
            outb                 write 8bits to I/O port
            writeb               write 8 bits to iomem
            inw                  read I/O port and returns 16bits
            readw                read iomem and return 16 bits
            outw                 write 16bits to I/O port
            writew               write 16 bits to iomem
            inl                  read I/O port and returns 32bits
            readl                read iomem and return 32 bits
            outl                 write 32bits to I/O port
            writel               write 32 bits to iomem
            readq                read iomem and return 64 bits
            writeq               write 64 bits to iomem

            /* unsigned char/short/int read*(const volatile void __iomem *addr);
             * void write*(unsigned char/short/int val, volatile void __iomem *addr);
             */

            /**
             * on x86,the difference between in*()/write*() and read*()/write*()
             *   read* and write* are implemented via "mov" instruction.
             *   in* and out* are implemented via "in*" and "out*" instruction.
             *                                    #^ inb/insb ...,outb/outsb ...
             * read*() and write*() usually is used for I/O Memory.
             * in*() and out*() usually is used for I/O Port.
             * for the architecutre that separate address space is featured for
             * peripheral device,we should use in*() and out*() for port I/O.
             */

            /* block I/O on I/O Memory */
            memcpy_fromio        @dst : void * <= @src : void __iomem *
                                 @count : size_t => size of data
            memcpy_toio          @dst : void __iomem * <= @src : void *
                                 @count : size_t => size of data
            memset_io            @addr : void __iomem * <= @val
                                 @count : memory units need to setup

            ! the annotation "__iomem" is defined as
                __attribute__((noderef, address_space(2)))
              it is used by Sparse tool -- static code checking code.
    
              "noderef" "address_space(2)"
                if we derefered the pointer directly has this annotation,
                Sparse will shows warnning message,and it is in
                "address space 2" -- the normal address space is 0.
  
                correct :
                  writeb(0, device->register.opcode);
                incorrect :
                  /* maybe the architecture needs additional steps before
                   * access to I/O Memory.
                   */
                  *(device->register.opcode) = 0;

                ! those annotations is useful only to Sparse,no effects to
                  GCC.
    
              the implementation of read*/write* is like this
                /**
                 * some architectures the memory mapped I/O stuff
                 * needs to be accessed differently,but for x86,
                 * we can read/write the memory location directly.
                 */
                static inline u8 readb(const volatile void __iomem *addr)
                {
                        /* x86 */
                        return *(const volatile u8 __force *) addr;
                              /* ^we do force conversion to u8 pointer */
                }
              "__force" <= __attribute__((force))
    
            ! kernel also provided "string version" of in*() and out*(),such
              insb(),outsb().
              but has no reads*() and writes().
              for x86 platform,the ins and outs version are implemented via
              "rep" prefix,the counter is stored into RCX register.
              the generic implementation is included in <asm-generic/io.h>,
              in which those routines are implemented via a loop.
              NOTE,insb() is different from memcpy_fromio(),the target of
              insb() is a single 1 byte memory unit -- the port.
            ! kernel also provided "pause version" of in*() and out*(),such
              inb_p(),outb_p().
              if peripheral device is too slower than processor's clock,
              when the CPU transfers data too fast,the device may misses some
              data.
              to prevent such problem,"_p" version will insert slow_down_io()
              after an I/O instruction.
              slow_down_io() relies on native_io_delay(),which routine can be
              implemented via several ways :
                1> transfer 1 byte(in AL) to 0x80 port
                2> transfer 1 byte(in AL) to 0xed port
                3> udelay(2) /* delay 2ms */

                # 0x80 => POST
                  0xed => alternative POST
              NOTE,no such functions read*_p(),write*_p() are defined.

          from userspace
            if we want to access to I/O ports from userspace,must set access
            permission for our thread.
            the system call ioperm() can set permissions for us.
            <sys/io.h>
              /**
               * ioperm - set port I/O permissions
               * @from:   the port address to start,usually is the port number
               * @num:    the number of bits need to set
               * @turn_on:
               *          nonzero => set bits
               *          zero    => clear bits
               * return:  0 OR error code
               * # NEED CAP_SYS_RAWIO
               * # this routine sets permission bits,but it start from port
               *   address,the range is defined by @from and @num.
               *   range => [@from, @from + @num - 1]
               *   each port in the range corresponds to 1 permission bit.
               */
              int ioperm(unsigned long from, unsigned long num, int turn_on);

              ! another iopl() system call been deprecated,thus we do not list
                it.
              ! some helpers such inb(),inb_p(),outb(),outb_p(),insb(),outsb(),
                etc,defined in the same header.
                they are defined as inline macros,thus we must compile program
                with "-O" or "-O2",otherwise will cause link time error.
                suffix 'p' means program will pause until I/O complete.
                suffix 's' means sequence I/O,it receives an array and size of
                buffer.
                'b' 'w' 'l' means data width.

            ! some host platform has no ioperm() and iopl(),userspace can still
              access I/O ports by using '/dev/port' device file,however,that
              file is very platform-specific and not likely useful for anything
              but the PC.
              /* usually,x86-specific */

            e.g.
              if (ioperm(0x0060, 2, 1) < 0) {
                      fprintf(stderr, "error: failed to set port permissions\n");
                      return -EPERM;
              }

              uint8_t b = 0;
              b = inb_p(0x0060);
              fprintf(stdout, "info: inb on 0x0060 returned %hhu\n", b);

          platform dependencies
            I/O port read/write is highly depends on platform.
    
            for example,x86 supports 64-kB I/O space,the type port parameter
            in the routines are "unsigned short",but for other platforms,
            they are "unsigned long",because where ports are just special
            locations in the same address space as memory.
    
            another example,"Alpha" architecture supports all I/O functions,
            and ports are memory-mapped,but the implementation of port I/O
            is different in different "Alpha" platforms,according to the
            chipset they use.ports are "unsigned long".

            "ARM",ports are memory-mapped,all functions are supported,string
            functions are implemented in C,ports are "unsigned int".

          parallel interface
            /* in minimal configuration(ECP and EPP modes)
             * come with three 8-bit ports.
             */
            the PC standard starts the I/O ports for the first parallel interface
            at (memory location)0x378 and for the second at 0x278.
            0x378 is bidirectional data register,it connects directly to pins
            2 -- 9 on the physical connector.
            0x278 is a read-only status register.
            the 3rd port is an output-only control register.

            signal levels used in parallel communications
              standard TTL(transistor-transistor logic) 0 -- 5 volts,
              logic threshold at about 1.2 volts.

            ! parallel connector is not isolated from the computer's internal
              circuitry.
              wiring correctly is very important,otherwise it will damages
              the motherboard.

        Using I/O Memory
          depending on the platform and bus being used,I/O memory may or may
          not be accessed through page tables.
    
          through page tables,kernel needs to arrange for the physical address
          to be visible from driver,thus we must call ioremap() before doing
          any I/O operations.
    
          if no page tables,the I/O Memory locations look like I/O Ports.

          /* we should use wrappers rather than direct dereference the
           * I/O Memory locations.
           * -- even x86 supports direct pointer dereference on I/O Memory
           *    locations
           */

          allocation
            <linux/ioport.h>
              /**
               * request_mem_region - request I/O Memory region
               * @start:              starting address
               * @len:                region length
               * @name:               name identifier
               * return:              resource descriptor OR NULL
               * # different from request I/O port,we request resource
               *   from @iomem_resource,it is the system resource tree
               *   for I/O Memory.
               *   @ioport_resource is used for I/O Ports.
               */
              #define request_mem_region(start, n, name) \
                      __request_region(&iomem_resource, (start), (n), (name), 0)

              /* exclusive version,request userland may not map this resource */
              #define request_mem_region_exclusive(start, n, name) \
                      __request_region(&iomem_resource, (start), (n), (name), \
                                       IORESOURCE_EXCLUSIVE)

          release
            <linux/ioport.h>
              /* release resouce */
              #define release_mem_region(start, n) \
                      __release_region(&iomem_resource, (start), (n))

          checking
            <linux/ioport.h>
              /* check whether the resouce is available
               * ! check and allocation is not an atomic operation.
               */
              #define check_mem_region(start, n) \
                      __check_region(&iomem_resource, (start), (n))

          remap
            not all platform supports direct pointer dereference on I/O Memory,
            thus we may need to make the locations are visible to driver.
            so,we need to map it at first.

            function ioremap() can be used to map bus memory into memory space
            that CPU can accesses via mmio helpers(includes inb/outb/readb ...).
            the primary work is hands over to __ioremap_caller(),which remap an
            arbitrary physical address space into the kernel linear address
            space.
            /* ioremap_page_range() is called to modify Kernel Page Table
             * entries.
             */
            function iounmap() can be used to unmap the I/O Memory that has
            been mapped.

            ! we can get ioremap information from /proc/iomem .
            ! even we have remapped the I/O memory,we still need to use the
              accessor functions instead directly dereference pointer.
            ! ioremap_nocache() can ensure the memory is not hardware cached.

          accessing
            <lib/iomap.c>
              /**
               * IO_COND - this macro is needed by ioread* and iowrite*
               *           it convert @addr to unsigned long,saves result
               *           in local @port.
               *           if @port >= PIO_RESERVED => Memory I/O
               *           if @port > PIO_OFFSET => Port I/O
               *           otherwise,bad_io_access().
               */
              #define IO_COND(addr, is_pio, is_mmio)

              /**
               * ioread* - read on remapped I/O memory
               * # rely on IO_COND()
               *     8bit => @is_pio == return inb(port)
               *             @is_mmio == return readb(addr)
               *     ...
               */
              unsigned int ioread8(void __iomem *addr);
              unsigned int ioread16(void __iomem *addr);
              unsigned int ioread32(void __iomem *addr);
              unsigned int ioread32be(void __iomem *addr);

              /* similar to ioread*,but for write,relies
               * on IO_COND(),and out*(),write*()
               */
              unsigned int iowrite8(void __iomem *addr);
              unsigned int iowrite16(void __iomem *addr);
              unsigned int iowrite32(void __iomem *addr);
              unsigned int iowrite32be(void __iomem *addr);

              /* repeat version */
              void ioread*_rep(void __iomem *addr, void *dst, unsigned long count);
              void iowrite*_rep(void __iomem *addr, const void *dst,
                                unsigned long count);

              /* block I/O on remapped I/O memory relies on
               *   memcpy_fromio
               *   memcpy_toio
               *   memset_io
               */

        Ports as I/O Memory
          we can create virtual mapping that maps port to iomem.
    
          <lib/iomap.c>
            /**
             * encode the physical Port I/O address(0 -- 0xffff) into
             * the pointer by offsetting them with a constant(0x10000)
             * and assuming that all the low addresses are always
             * Port I/O.
             */
            #define PIO_OFFSET 0x10000UL
            #define PIO_MASK   0x0ffffUL
            #define PIO_RESERVED 0x40000UL
    
            /**
             * ioport_map - map I/O Port to "I/O Memory" location
             * @port:       port number
             * @nr:         number of ports to map
             * return:      I/O Memory address OR NULL
             * # if @port greater than PIO_MASK,NULL would be returned,
             *   otherwise,returns
             *     (void __iomem *)(unsigned long)(@port + PIO_OFFSET)
             * # we can access them via ioread*() and iowrite*() helpers.
             * # just make I/O ports look like memory,but the ports still
             *   needed to be allocated with request_region().
             */
            void __iomem *ioport_map(unsigned long port, unsigned int nr);

            /* NULL Body,does nothing */
            void ioport_unmap(void __iomem *addr);

        ISA Memory Below 1MB
          /* ISA -- Industry Standard Architecture */
          ISA I/O Memory appears almost all PC,the memory range is
            [640kB, 1MB]
            0xa0000 -- 0x100000
            size is 384kB
          the memory region belongs to "non-directly-mapped class of memory".
          /** 
           * ISA space is "always mapped' on a typical x86 system,no need to
           * explicitly ioremap() it.
           * in header <arch/x86/include/asm/io.h>,a macro definition
           *   __ISA_IO_base ((char __iomem *)(PAGE_OFFSET))
           * is exists,and the ISA space is start from PAGE_OFFSET in the
           * kernel space,as the virtual linear address.
           *
           * the macro ISA_START_ADDRESS is defined to 0xa0000,
           * and macro ISA_END_ADDRESS is defined to 0x100000.
           * if ISA space is not mapped,we need to ioremap() it before make use
           * of the memory region.
           * # header is <arch/x86/include/asm/e820.h>
           *   -- as physical address
           * # E820 BIOS routine is a standard x86 firmware interface used
           *   during system boot in real mode to query the physical
           *   System Address Map.
           *   kernel does early printk these mapping information,usually
           *   we can get it through "dmesg".
           * ! ISA Space is used by the old devices,modern devices can
           *   remap their ports,thus a kernel configure option allows us
           *   to get the ISA Space away from memory mapping.
           *   # such as sound cards,early video graphic adapters,etc.
           *
           * in source <arch/x86/kernel/setup.c>,the physical address region
           * start from 0xa0000,end at 0xbffff,devoted to "Video RAM area".
           * # described through a resource descriptor,named
           *     video_ram_resource
           *
           * for the Xen CPU,init function xen_memory_setup() will add a
           * memory region spanned the ISA space with type
           * E820_RESERVED,through routine e820_add_region().
           * # source is <arch/x86/xen/setup.c>
           *
           * for old PC with old kernel version,we may need to ioremap()
           * 0xa0000 at first,but modern kernel and PC do not need to
           * invoke ioremap(),kernel handles this automatically
           * at booting phase.
           */

          ! ISA I/O Memory exists only in x86-class computers.
          ! the old routines such isa_read* isa_write* been removed from
            kernel,instead,we should use the I/O Memory accessor functions
            to access ISA space.
            /* kernel 2.4/2.5,removed those routines */

Interrupt :    
        Autodetecting the IRQ Number
          driver needs to determine which IRQ line is going to be used by the
          device.in order to install ISR correctly for the device,this
          information is necessary to device driver.
          some devices feature a default behavior,driver can assumes that
          the default values apply.
          /**
           * e.g.
           *   if no IRQ line is specified at module loading
           *     default IRQ line is 7 if I/O address is 0x378
           *     default IRQ line is 2 if I/O address is 0x278
           *     default IRQ line is 5 if I/O address is 0x3bc
           *   # parallel port.
           */
          some devices can tell us which IRQ line they are going to use.
          driver can retrieves the number by read a status byte from one of
          the registers of device,or from PCI configuration space.
          /* probing */
          /* if the device resides on PCI bus,the standard requires it
           * declares which IRQ line it is going to use.
           */

          probe interrupt
            driver tells the device to generate interrupts and watches what
            happens.

            kernel-assisted probing
              <kernel/irq/autoprobe.c>
                /**
                 * probe_irq_on - begin an interrupt autodetect
                 * return:        a bit mask of unassigned interrupts,such
                 *                interrupts' descriptors have IRQ_AUTODETECT
                 *                is set
                 * # EXPORT_SYMBOL
                 * # this function should comes with probe_irq_off()
                 */
                unsigned long probe_irq_on(void);

                description for probe_irq_on() :
                  first,this routine invoke async_synchronize_full() to sync
                  or asynchronous tasks,then acquire mutex @probing_active.
                  next,executes three loops.
                  /**
                   * in the loop1,we traverse the IRQ lines from the tail to
                   * head.the front IRQ lines usually is non-maskable
                   * interrupts and architecture-specific interrupts.
                   * we want to probe a free IRQ line our device may going
                   * to use,such IRQ line has no irqaction registered.
                   * for each such IRQ line,the startup() method of the
                   * chip will enabled this IRQ line.
                   */
                  loop1 for_each_irq_desc_reverse()
                    for each IRQ line(IRQ vector,the total number is determined
                    by NR_IRQS in global variable @nr_irqs),if the IRQ line's
                    descriptor's @action is NULL AND @status disabled IRQ_NOPROBE
                    we invoke the @chip's startup() function,pass it current
                    IRQ line(index start from 0).
                    /* before if-statement test,we need to hold the descriptor's
                     * @lock member,and release it before next iteration.
                     * for old-style architecture,compat_irq_chip_set_default_handler()
                     * will be called.
                     * if the chip need to know about probing in progress,need to
                     * call @chip's set_type() method,pass it the IRQ line and
                     * flag IRQ_TYPE_PROBE.
                     */
                  call msleep() to wait 20ms.
                  /**
                   * in loop2,we set kernel IRQ line probing flags,the
                   * IRQ_AUTODETECT and IRQ_WAITING.
                   * the flow handler of interrupt will always disables
                   * IRQ_WAITING in its execution.
                   * we set those flags,then if there is such IRQ line raised
                   * an interrupt,and its IRQ_WAITING will be clear.
                   */
                  loop2 for_each_irq_desc_reverse()
                    for each descriptor has no @action AND disabled IRQ_NOPROBE,
                    we enable IRQ_AUTODETECT | IRQ_WAITING on its @status.
                    invoke the chip's startup() with current IRQ line,if the
                    result is TRUE,we set IRQ_PENDING on its @status.
                    /* startup() returned non-zero,the interrupt is pending */
                  msleep 100ms.
                  /**
                   * in loop3,we traverse IRQ lines no irqaction registered from
                   * head to tail,because we would need to setup the mask in
                   * a right order.
                   * IRQ_AUTODETECT and IRQ_WAITING is set in loop2,if there is
                   * a IRQ line that raised interrupt,and IRQ_WAITING is cleared,
                   * that means spurious interrupt happened,because the probe
                   * window starts after probe_irq_on() returned.
                   * anyway,the spurious interrupts are filter out,and we
                   * shutdown() the IRQ lines,it will not effect our probe
                   * window.
                   */
                  loop3 for_each_irq_desc()
                    if current descriptor has IRQ_AUTODETECT is set,we continue
                    to test whether IRQ_WAITING is disabled,if it is,we disable
                    IRQ_AUTODETECT of it,and invoke its chip's shutdown() method
                    on the IRQ line.
                    IRQ_WAITING still set,we set the corresponding bit in the mask
                    to current IRQ line.
                    /* @mask is modified if and only if current IRQ line less than
                     * 32 -- potential interrupts.
                     * usually,vectors 0 -- 19 devote to NMI and Exceptions,
                     * and vectors 20 -- 31 devote to architecture-specific use.
                     * the external interrupts' vectors start from 32.
                     */
                  finally,return the mask.

                /**
                 * probe_irq_off - end an interrupt autodetect
                 * @val:           mask of potential interrups
                 *                 (unused)
                 * return:         the IRQ line number that interrupt raised on,
                 *                 or a negative value which is the first
                 *                 candidate to more than one IRQ lines on them
                 *                 interrupt is found
                 * # EXPORT_SYMBOL
                 * # the interrupt probe logic state is returned to its previous
                 *   value.
                 * ! BUGS
                 *     when used in a module(which arguably should not happen)
                 *     nothing prevents two IRQ probe callers from overlapping,
                 *     the results of this are non-optimal.
                 */
                int probe_irq_off(unsigned long val);

                description for probe_irq_off() :
                  we invoke this routine later after probe_irq_on() returned.
                  probe_irq_on() and probe_irq_off() defined a probing window
                  for interrupt.
                  this function traverse irq_desc(s) from 0 to NR_IRQS,for each
                  irq_desc object
                    if its @status enabled IRQ_AUTODETECT -- set by probe_irq_on(),
                    we continue to test if IRQ_WAITING is cleared.
                    both are TRUE,then we found a IRQ line our device may using.
                    we set local variable @irq_found to current IRQ line index,
                    if the local recorder @nr_of_irqs is _zero_,because there may
                    be more than one IRQ line raised interrupt.
                    we increase @nr_of_irqs whenever we found an interrupt.
                    next,set clear IRQ_AUTODETECT in this IRQ line's @status,
                    invoke chip->shutdown() on it to disable it.
                  when the loop ends,we test @nr_of_irqs,if it greater than 1,
                  that means we found several IRQ lines,so we minus the
                  @irq_found.
                  finally,return @irq_found to caller.

                Usage :
                  kernel-driver :
                    unsigned long mask = 0;
                    int irqline = 0;
                    ...
                    /* @mask is useless for this probe */
                    mask = probe_irq_on();          <-----------+ 
                    ... /* we command the peripheral device     | irq probe
                         * to raise an interrupt in this        | window
                         * probe window.                        |
                         * # through I/O Ports                  |
                         */                                     |
                    irqline = probe_irq_off(mask);  <-----------+
                    if (irqline < 0) {
                            printk(KERN_ERR "more than one interrupts raised"
                                   " in probe window.");
                            return -EAGAIN;
                    }

            do it by ourselves
              the mechanism is the same
                -- enable all unused interrupts,then wait and see what happens.
              device usually can be configured to use one IRQ line number from
              a set of three or four,we can just monitor those IRQs instead to
              probe all unused IRQs.

              instead to install real ISR,we install some probe routines to the
              possible IRQ lines.
              then we let the device raises an interrupt,the probe routine needs
              to record the IRQ line number it actually seen.we can get the
              real IRQ line through read the recorder.

              e.g.
                int real_irq = 0;

                static irqreturn_t probe_IRQ(int irq, void *dev_id)
                {
                        real_irq = irq;
                        return IRQ_HANDLED;
                }

                int possible_irqs[] = {3, 5, 7, 9};
                for (unsigned idx = 0; idx < sizeof(possible_irqs) / sizeof(int);
                     ++idx)
                        if (request_irq(possible_irqs[idx], probe_IRQ,
                                        IRQF_SAMPLE_RANDOM, "irq probe", NULL)
                            < 0) {
                                printk(KERN_ERR "request irq for probing failed.");
                                goto err_exit;
                        }
                ... /* command device to raise interrupt */
                ... /* wait a while -- udelay */
                if (real_irq <= 0) {
                        printk(KERN_ERR "failed to probe irq.");
                        goto err_exit;
                }
                ...
                err_exit:
    
                undo_req_irq(possible_irqs);
                return real_irq <= 0 ? -1 : 0;

        Disable IRQ line
          kernel allows we to disable single IRQ line or disable all IRQ on
          local CPU.

          disable single irq
            disable_irq(@irq)
            disable_irq_nosync(@irq)
            enable_irq(@irq)

          disable all irq /* "cli" and "sti" instructions */
            local_irq_disable()
            local_irq_enable()
            local_irq_save(@flags)
            local_irq_restore(@flags)   /* @flags => EFLAGS */

        Interrupt Sharing
          linux supports irq sharing on all buses(even ISA bus),to avoid
          irq conflict.
          we can request an IRQ line that might shared with others through
            1> pass flag IRQF_SHARED.
            2> argument @dev_id must not be NULL and need to be unique.
          the result depends on
            1> the IRQ line is free.
            2> all handlers already registered for this line also specified
               IRQF_SHARED.

          ! when driver needs to unregister its ISR from the shared IRQ line,
            it must pass @dev_id to free_irq().
          ! for the shared IRQ line,driver cant call disable_irq() on it,
            because it is sharing the IRQ line,not owns it.
    
          the requirement that ISR needs to follow
            1> @dev_id as a signature,if the interrupt should not be handled
               by this ISR,must quickly exit with IRQ_NONE.


          about shared IRQ probing
            no probing function is available for shared handlers.
            /* modern hardware designed for interrupt sharing is able to
             * tell the processor which interrupt it is using,thus
             * eliminating the need for explicit probing.
             */

          irqaction list
            kernel add new ISR to the end of irq_desc->action,the @next member
            of struct irqaction can get into next entry.
            this happens only when the IRQ line is allowed to be shared.

          ISRs on shared IRQ line
            the ISRs registered on this shared IRQ line,they will be called
            by handle_IRQ_event().that function makes use of a loop to
            iterates each irqaction on the list,and invoke @handler member
            of current irqaction entry.

          determine whether or not current interrupt must handled by us
            we can check the @dev_id passed by handle_IRQ_event(),or
            we can do port I/O in ISR to ask our device if it just raised
            an interrupt.

          handling
            at the top halves,ISR should quickly check and ack current
            interrupt,the time-cost works should be handed over to
            the bottom halves,kernel schedules the task in future.
            e.g.
              static void bottom_fn(unsigned long data)
              {
                      int v = *((int *)data);
                      printk(KERN_INFO "device has sent %d .", v);
              }

              static irqreturn_t the_ISR(int irq, void *dev_id)
              {
                      if (dev_id != driver_dev_id_addr)
                              return IRQ_NONE;
                      outb(0x01, device_irq_ack_port);
                      device_data_buffer = inl(device_irq_data_port);
                      tasklet_schedule(driver_bottom_tasklet);
                      return IRQ_HANDLED;
              }

        Interrupt-Driven I/O
          usually,we provides data buffers for peripheral device,they
          can help to detach data transmission and reception from
          the write and read system calls.
            input buffer : the buffer stores data from device
            output buffer : the buffer stores data from write syscall
          when ISR starts execution,it needs to
            if device sent some data,the data should be stored into
            input buffer,and readed by userspace process through
            read system call later.
            /* if the buffer is empty,and read is sleeping,then
             * we needs to wake-up the process.
             * if we hand over the task to tasklet,the bottom
             * needs fill the input buffer,and wake-up that process.
             */
    
            next,empty the output buffer from userspace,send them
            to peripheral device,this usually is done via DMA.
            /* we may also need to wake-up the process that is
             * blocked on write system call because there is no
             * room for new data.
             */

          interrupt-driven I/O on hardware
            1> for input,the device needs to interrupt the processor
               when new data has arrived,and is ready to be retrieved
               by the system processor.
               /* through I/O ports,I/O Memory,DMA */
            2> for output,the device dlivers an interrupt either when
               it is ready to accept new data or to acknowledge a
               successful data transfer.
               /* memory-mapped and DMA-capable devices usually
                * generate interrupts to tell the system they are done
                * with the buffer.
                */

          diagram
            [ user process ]     [ kernel ]              [ ISR ]        [ device ]
                  |                     |                   |               |
                  V                     |                   |               |
                read                    |                   |               |
                  |                     V                   |               |
                  |                driver mark input        |               |
                  |                request                  |               |
                  |                     |                   |               V
                  |                     |                   |             data ready
                  |                     |                   |               |
                  |                     |                   |               V
                  |                     |                   |             interrupt
                  |                     |                   |               |
                  |                     |                   v               |
                  |                     |                  ACK              |
                  |                     |                   |               V
                  |                     |                   V              idle
                  |                     |                 handle
                  |                     |                   |
                  |                     |                   V
                  |                     |                 schedule
                  |                     |                 bottom
                  |                     |                 halves
                  |                     |                 task
                  |                     |                   |
                  |                     |                   V
                  |                     |                 return
                  |                     |                 IRQ_HANDLED
                  |                     |
                  |                     V
                  |                delayed function
                  |                start
                  |                     |
                  |                     V
                  |                read data from
                  |                I/O Memory
                  |                     |
                  |                     V
                  |                fill input buffer
                  |                     |
                  |                     V
                  |                clear input request
                  |                mark
                  |                     |
                  |                     V
                  |                wake up process
                  |                     |
                  |                     V
                  |                schedule
                  V
               woken up
                  |
                  V
               copy_to_user
                  |
                  V
               return from
               syscall

            ! write is the similar case.for interrupt driven-I/O,we just need
              to require the ISR does additional output buffer handle as well
              -- schedule bottom havles task that empty output buffer and send
              data to peripheral device.
              when the data transmission finished,the device may will raises
              an interrupt to tell this truth,we can check both buffers in
              this interrupt,and do the same tasks.
            ! the input request mark just an assumption,not really need.
              usually,the block-read should to wait until data is ready.
              if the peripheral device is a network card,we do not know the
              real time data will come in,thus request the device for input
              is unrealistic.
              we can request the device to send data such as it is a
              random number generator connected to the computer.in this case,
              read system call actually like a request,from view point of
              programming,it is a calling to random().
                long v = random();
                printk(KERN_INFO "value is %ld .", v);
                
Linux Device Model :
        abstractions
          power management and system shutdown
          communications with user space
          hotpluggable devices
          device classes
          object lifecycles

        kobject
          kobject is the fundamental structure holds the device model together.
          it takes care of
            object lifecycle        
            sysfs entry             
            subsystem               
            model hierarchy         
            hotplug event handling
          this object usually is embedded into other device model to let the
          device get into linux device model.
          /* like an abstracted base class */

          allocation
            use kmalloc() or other memory allocation functions.because kobject
            usually is embedded into other structure,this should be done by
            the corresponding allocator.
            if we are design a device and we need to create a new kobject for
            the device descriptor,we can
              > just use kobject_create() to create it.
                /* kobject_create() allocates it via kzalloc(),and invokes
                 * kobject_init() on it.
                 */
              > use kernel memory allocation function,and init it through
                kobject_init(),etc.

          deallocation
            been initialized
              must use kobject_put()
            have not been initialized /* before kobject_init() called */
              use kfree() if it is created via kmalloc(),kzalloc(),etc.
    
          init step
            kobject_init() /* set kref to 1 */

            /* may failed because memory allocation failed(for @name field) */
            kobject_set_name()

          get and put
            kobject_get()
            kobject_put() /* must be called if kobject_init() is called */

          ! get a reference of kobject,usually corresponds to get references
            of its container and the module who created the container.
            if the kobject is still being passed around,the module would not
            unload.
            /* module put is later than kobject put */

          release
            kobject_put() will invoke kref_put(),the callback passed to the
            routine is kobject_release(),which function actually calls to
            kobject_cleanup().
            because the different types of kobjects' containers may have
            different releasing manner,thus the real release task is handed
            over to the @release method of kobj_type structure,kobject has
            a member named @ktype which points to such data structure.
            the general release steps /* executed by kobject_cleanup() */
              1> send uevent about KOBJ_REMOVE if necessary
              2> invoke kobject_del() if the kobject been registered in
                 sysfs
              3> invoke release() member of @ktype if such method is
                 implemented
              4> kfree() @name if it is not NULL
            ! kref_put() calls to callback if and only if the refcount
              decreased to _zero_.

            ! if our device descriptor holds a kobject,we must pay attention
              to kobject's reference count
                1> dev_get() also need to invoke kobject_get().
                2> dev_put() also need to invoke kobject_put().

                refcount becomes _zero_
                  when refcount becomes 0,the release() of @ktype is called.
                  we can let the implementation to deallocates the device
                  descriptor together
                    void __dev_free(struct kobject *kobj)
                    {
                            struct TheDev *devp = container_of(kobj,
                                                              struct TheDev,
                                                              kobj);
                            ... /* other release steps */
    
                            kfree(devp);
                    }
                    /**
                     * we let the device descriptor to be deallocated when
                     * kobject's refcount become 0.
                     * if we make use of this strategy,we can simply let
                     * the dev_get() and dev_put() become wrappers of
                     * kobject_get() and kobject_put(),respectively.
                     * in other word,struct TheDev does not provide an
                     * additional refcounter,because kobject has
                     * featured it.
                     */

                    /* the cdev's kobject release method is
                     *   # for location allocation,no kfree() called
                     *   cdev_default_release()
                     *   # for dynamic allocation,kfree() called to
                     *     free the cdev descriptor
                     *   cdev_dynamic_release()
                     *
                     *   -> p = container_of(kobj, struct cdev, kobj);
                     *      ...
                     *      kfree(p);
                     */

        Kobject Hierarchies and Ksets
          kobject is often used to link together objects into a hierarchical
          structure that matches the structure of the subsystem being modeled.
          @parent member of kobject points to another kobject that is the
          up one level to this kobject.

          Ksets
            a kset is a collection of kobjects embedded within structures of the
            same type.

            /* kobj_type => for the type of an object
             * kset      => for collection of kobjects of objects have same type
             * # kset like the top-level container class for kobjects,and kset
             *   contains its own kobject internally.
             */

            add kobject to kset
              /**
               * @kobj->parent set to @parent or @kobj->kset->kobj
               * @kobj->name set to resolved @fmt
               * if @kobj->kset is not NULL,kobj_kset_join() to let @kobj joins
               * @kobj->kset.
               * create sysfs dentry.
               * # increase parent's refcounter
               *   increase kset's refcounter
               */
              kobject_add(@kobj, @parent, @fmt, ...)

              /* been removed in 2.6.34.1 */
              kobject_register()

            remove kobject from kset
              /**
               * @kobj->parent set to NULL
               * kobj_kset_leave() called to leave from the kset
               * # decrease parent's refcounter
               *   decrease kset's refcounter
               */
              kobject_del(@kobj)

              /* been removed in 2.6.34.1 */
              kobject_unregister()

            ! kobject->parent may points to kobject->kset->kobj,or points to
              another kobject,if we did kobject_add().

            ! ktype for a kset is represented by the kset->kobj->ktype,kset
              has not @ktype member.
            ! kset's ktype is used to describing the kobjects it contains.
            ! if we want to add a kobject to a kset,we must initialize its
              @kset member before invoke kobject_add().
              for example,a kobject can set its @kset to @module_kset,if its
              container is a kernel module.

            dynamicly creation
              /**
               * @parent_kobj is NULL also be okay,the kobject_add_internal()
               * will select the kset's internal @kobj's kset,if both are NULL,
               * that means no parent.in this case,the kset dir would be created
               * at the sysfs's root -- /sysfs/
               * ! the kset's internal @kobj must has name setup.
               */
              kset_create(@name, @uevent_ops, @parent_kobj)

            init kset
              /* kobject_init() the kset's internal @kobj */
              kset_init()

            register kset
              /* kobject_add_internal() the kset's internal @kobj */
              kset_register()

            unregister kset
              /* just kobject_put() the kset's internal @kobj */
              kset_unregister()

            get and put
              kset_get()
              kset_put()

            release
              kset_release() /* should not be called directly,instead,kset_put()
                              * will calls to this routine if refcounter becomes
                              * _zero_
                              */

            !! "subsystem" STRUCTURE IS A COLLECTION OF "kset"(s),AND IT BEEN
               REMOVED SINCE 2.6.22 .
                 /sys/bus/pci/drivers/serial/new_id
                  |   |   |   |       |      |
                  |   |   |   |       |      |
                  |   |   |   |       |      +-> attribute
                  |   |   |   |       +-> kobject
                  |   |   |   +-> kset
                  |   |   +-> subsystem
                  |   +-> subsystem
                  +-> sysfs root

               ! subsystem not always show up in sysfs.

               IF THERE IS NO SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS,SHOULD NOT ADD NEW SUBSYSTEM
               INTO KERNEL.

               ! cgroup is introduced since 2.6.24.each subsystem needs to
                 declares itself in <linux/cgroup_subsys.h> if cgroup is
                 supported.
                   SUBSYS(blkio)
                   => extern struct cgroup_subsys blkio_subsys;
                   SUBSYS(_x) => extern struct cgroup_subsys _x ## _subsys;
                   /* <linux/cgroup.h> */

        Low-Level sysfs Operations
          sysfs is a in-memory virtual filesystem,it describes the linux
          device model,the most important component is kobject.
            /sys /* top level @sysfs_root */
            |
            +---- block/
            |
            +---- bus/
            |
            +---- class/
            |
            +---- dev/
            |
            +---- devices/
            |
            +---- firmware/
            |
            +---- fs/
            |
            +---- kernel/
            |     |
            |     +-------- address_bits    /* attribute */
            |     |
            |     +-------- boot_params/
            +---- module/
            |
            +---- power/

                  /* subsystems,and each contains a kobject in its
                   * kernel data structure.
                   */

          "attribute" is a file that contains kernel-generated informations.
          a kobject has a member named @sd,it is a pointer to sysfs dir entry.
          in which structure,some attribute related members are exist.
          the directories are created through function create_dir(), which is
          called by kobject_add().

          1> the sysfs entry for a kobject always a directory.
          2> the name of kobject is used as the name of directory.
             because the dentries under the same parent directory,can not have
             the same name,thus kobjects in the hierarchy under the same
             parent kobject must have unique names.
             # name cant contains slash,and should not contains white space.
          3> parent directory of the kobject is ->parent,if it is NULL,then
             it is kset->kobj->parent;both are NULL then it is top level
             @sysfs_root .

        default attributes
          kobject->ktype->default_attrs is an array of pointers to struct
          attribute objects that represents default attributes to this
          kobject,it ends with NULL pointer.
          member @sysfs_ops of kobj_type structure can be used to implement
          these attributes.
            /* e.g.
             *   the method show() of sysfs_ops usually is called in the
             *   default read implementation of sysfs.
             *   # through sysfs_buffer structue.
             *   at opposite,method store() of sysfs_ops is called in
             *   write system call.
             */

          ! the 3rd parameter to show() is type of char * which points
            to buffer provided by kernel,the size is PAGE_SIZE.
          ! the 3rd parameter to store() is type of const char * which
            points to kernel buffer,and the 4th parameter is the length of
            data in the buffer,usually the maximum length is PAGE_SIZE.
            the data is received from userspace,we must validate it
            at first.
          ! show() and store() is called on all attributes for I/O,
            thus the implementation needs to determine which attribute
            now is request.
            e.g.
              ssize_t the_show_method(struct kobject *kobj,
                                      struct attribute *attr,
                                      char *buffer)
              {
                      ssize_t length = 0;
                      enum ATTR_ID attr_id = ATTR_NONEXIST;
                      attr_id = resolve_attr_id(kobj, attr);
                      switch (attr_id) {
                      case ATTR_MODULE_NAME:
                              length = store_module_name(buffer);
                              break;
                      /* ... for other attributes */
                      default:
                              return 0;
                      }
                      return length;
              }

              /**
               * because the elements in @default_attrs are pointers,
               * we can wrap the attribute structure into a meaningful
               * structure,and use the container_of() macro to extract
               * the container,in which we use some fields to store
               * the value should be returned by show() for this
               * attribute.
               *   struct attr_wrapper {
               *           struct attribute attr_id;
               *           ssize_t (*get_value)(char *buffer);    
               *           const char value[64];
               *   };
               *
               *   struct attr_wrapper *wrapper
               *           = container_of(attr, struct attr_wrapper, attr_id);
               *   ssize_t length = wrapper->get_value(buffer);
               *   # we can assign different implementation to @get_value,
               *     then we do not need to distinguish the actual attr
               *     via string-compare or hash function.
               *     the struct attr_wrapper is like an abstract base class.
               */

          attribute->name points to a C-style string,it will be the name
          of regular file that corresponds to this attribute.
    
          attribute->mode is the file mode for that regular file.
          /* usually is S_IRUGO for read-only;S_IWUSR for write-only.
           *                                 #^ only root writible
           */
    
          attribute->owner is a pointer to module structure,points to the
          module implements this attribute.(owns this kobject)

          ! convention
              sysfs state that each attribute should contain a single,
              human-readable value.
              # if there is more information,we should split it into
                other attributes.

        non-default attributes    
          except the default attributes,kernel also allows add new attributes
          to the kobject.
          /* but those new attributes are handled by show() and store()
           * methods,too.
           */

          there are three kinds of additional attributes
            1> regular file
            2> binary attribute
            3> symbolic link

          regular attribute
            add
              sysfs_create_file()
            remove
              /* unlink until the last opened file closed */
              sysfs_remove_file()

            the regular attribute as same as the default attribute,just a
            regular file would be created under the kobject's directory.

          binary attribute
            a file also created by kernel,but it acts like a pipe used to
            transfer binary data from userspace to kernel space,or from
            kernel space to userspace.
            /* there is no synchronous mechanism can takes place,the user
             * program needs to take charge of that.
             */

            different from regular attribute,the I/O on binary attribute
            is handled by read() and write() methods that contained in
            the bin_attribute structure.

            <linux/sysfs.h>
              /**
               * bin_attribute - the binary attribute in sysfs
               * @attr:          attribute identifier
               * @size:          the maximum chunk size of binary data
               *                 # 0 means no maximum
               * @private:       private data
               * @read:          method called when read from this attribute
               * @write:         method called when write to this attribute
               * @mmap:          method called when memory mapping on this
               *                 attribute
               * # bin_attribute likes a wrapper of attribute
               */
              struct bin_attribute {
                      struct attribute attr;
                      size_t size;
                      void *private;

                      /* 3rd param - buffer pointer
                       * 4th param - file offset
                       * 5th param - size of buffer
                       * return    - size of data been transferred
                       */                
                      ssize_t (*read)(struct kobject *, struct bin_attribute *,
                                      char *, loff_t, size_t);
                      ssize_t (*write)(struct kobject *, struct bin_attribute *,
                                       char *, loff_t, size_t);
    
                      int (*mmap)(struct kobject *, struct bin_attribute *attr,
                                  struct vm_area_struct *vma);
              };

            add
              sysfs_create_bin_file()
    
            remove
              sysfs_remove_bin_file()

          symbolic link attribute
            this type of attribute usually is used to reflects the relathionship
            between kobjects.
            for example
              /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:04:00.0
              |
              | <= a symbolic link
              |
              V
              # ../../../devices/pci0000:00/0000:00:1d.0/0000:04:00.0
              /sys/devices/pci0000:00/0000:00:1d.0/0000:04:00.0
    
            the symbolic link is the symlink attribute.

            add
              /* @kobj:        the parent directory
               * @target:      the target kobject
               * @name:        name of this being create dentry
               */
              sysfs_create_link(@kobj, @target, @name)

            remove
              sysfs_remove_link()

            ! even the target kobject is removed in sysfs,the symbolic link
              still persists.
              if we have a way to monitoring the target kobject's persistence,
              we can adjust the symlink attribute in real-time.

        hotplug event
          a hotplug event is a notification to user space from the kernel that
          something has changed in the system's configuration.
          they are generated whenever a kobject is created or destroyed.
          /**
           *  in Linux-2.6.34.1,kobject_add() would not invoke kobject_uevent()
           *  inside its body.this is handed over to implementation,after
           *  all sysfs dentries are created,the implementation should
           *  invokes kobject_uevent() with KOBJ_ADD to notify userspace.
           *  # caller should setup all of the necessary sysfs files for
           *    the object and then call kobject_uevent().
           *  different from kobject_add(),kobject_del() will invoke
           *  kobject_uevent() with KOBJ_REMOVE if caller did not
           *  send this event previously,this is done by kobject_cleanup().
           *
           *  order
           *    create sysfs file
           *    caller KOBJ_ADD
           *
           *    caller KOBJ_REMOVE
           *    remove sysfs file
           */

          ! in the early kernel version,the hotplug event is send through the
            methods defined in structure kset_hotplug_ops,and kset has a member
            named @hotplug_ops.
            in 2.6.34.1,the structure is changed to
              struct kset_uevent_ops
            and kset->uevent_ops points to the implementations.

          <linux/kobject.h>
            /**
             * kobj_uevent_env - kobject userspace event environment
             * @envp:            environment variables,pointers point to
             *                   @buf's locations
             * @envp_idx:        current end of @envp
             *                   # size of valid pointers in @envp
             * @buf:             string buffer for environment variables
             * @buflen:          all valid strings in @buf,includes the
             *                   '\0' of the string                  
             */
            struct kobj_uevent_env {
                    /* UEVENT_NUM_ENVP - 32 */
                    char *envp[UEVENT_NUM_ENVP];
                    int envp_idx;
                    /* UEVENT_BUFFER_SIZE - 2048 */
                    char buf[UEVENT_BUFFER_SIZE];
                    int buflen;
            };

            /**
             * kset_uevent_ops - userspace event operations
             * @filter:          called whenever kernel is considering
             *                   generating an event for a given kobject
             *                   # return 0 => event is not created
             *                   # kset code use this method to decide
             *                     which events should be passed to userspace
             *                     and which should not
             * @name:            usually returns a string describes the subsystem
             *                   this uevent comes from
             *                   # would be stored in environment
             *                       "SUBSYSTEM=$(name)"
             * @uevent:          used to add additional kset information to
             *                   the environment
             *                   # return 0 if succeed to add informations,
             *                     non-zero will let kobject_uevent() abort
             *                     generation to this uevent
             */
            struct kset_uevent_ops {
                    int (*const filter)(struct kset *kset, struct kobject *kobj);
                    const char *(* const name)(struct kset *kset, struct kobject *kobj);
                    int (* const uevent)(struct kset *kset, struct kobject *kobj,
                                         struct kobj_uevent_env *env);
            };

          kobject_uevent() finally invoke call_usermodehelper() to start a user
          process that handles the uevent send by kernel.
          the helper's path is "/sbin/hotplug" on x86 architecture.
          but newer distribution been migrated to "udev",thus the uevent is
          handled by udevd -- a User Mode daemon.

        Buses
          a bus is a channel between the processor and one or more devices.
                  CPU
                   |
                   |
                   |
            ===============
            | | | | | | | |
            d d d d d d d d   
            e e e e e e e e
            v v v v v v v v
            1 2 3 ....... N
                          |
                          +--> a USB controller
                          |
                          |
                        ===== <- USB bus

          all devices are connected via a bus,even if it is an internal,virtual
          "platform" bus.
          each buses can plug into each other. /* but no ring is allowed */

          bus_type
            a structure represents a type of bus.

          registeration/unregisteration
            bus_register()
            bus_unregister()

            /* if we succeed to register a new bus,it will appears at the
             * top-level of sysfs,in "devices" directory.that means
             * implicitly an actual bus is a device,and must be registered
             * separately.
             */

          bus methods
            ->match
              - method used to called when a new device or driver is added to
                the bus.
                it receives a device descriptor and a driver descriptor,if
                the device can be handled by the driver,nonzero value will
                be returned.

                /* the match method usually going to checks whether the
                 * device's id is included in the driver's device id table,
                 * if found,that means the device can be handled by this
                 * driver.
                 */

            ->uevent
              - method called when the bus needs to send event notification
                to userspace.

            ->probe
              - method called when a device connected to the bus and needs
                to probe bus driver for this device.

            ->remove
              - method to remove a device from the bus.

            ->shutdown
              - shutdown a device hosted on this bus.

            ->suspend
              - suspend a device.

            ->resume
              - resume a been suspended device.

          iteration on bus's devices and drivers
            int bus_for_each_dev(@bus, @start, @data, @fn)
            int bus_for_each_drv(@bus, @start, @data, @fn)

            these functions are not macro functions,so we can not use them
            like the list_for_each() macro.

            *_dev performs an iteration on @bus's devices,@start is a pointer
            points to struct device object.if it is not NULL,the iteration start
            from where,otherwise start from the first device.
            for current device,@fn is called on it,pass to it the device's
            descriptor and @data.
            *_drv is similar but perform on drivers.

            ! if @fn returned nonzero,the loop would stops,and the result is
              returned to the caller.

            ! in the early kernel version,those two routines will acquire
              bus subsystem's semaphore,but in 2.6.34.1,subsystem structure
              is no longer exists,thus bus_type has no member is type of
              subsytem.

          bus attributes
            member ->bus_attrs is type of struct bus_attribute *,this type just
            a wrapper of struct attribute,and which is defined in
            <linux/device.h>.
              struct bus_attribute {
                      struct attribute attr;
                      ssize_t (*show)(struct bus_type *bus, char *buf);
                      ssize_t ((store)(struct bus_type *bus, const char *buf,
                                       size_t count);
              };

            a macro function BUS_ATTR(@name, @mode, @show, @store) is defined
            in the same header,it is used to declare and initialize a local
            struct bus_attribute object.
            /* it relies on __ATTR() macro,it works like a generic initializer
             * to attribute wrapper has such form
             * {
             *         @attr        <= struct attribute
             *         @show        <= show method
             *         @store       <= store method
             * };
             */

            note that,member ->bus_attrs is a pointer,in other view point,it can
            be an array of struct bus_attribute objects.as the usual,the last
            element is used as a mark of end.
            the last element's @attr's @name member must be a NULL pointer.

            add sysfs file
              bus_create_file(@bus, @bus_attr)

            remove sysfs file
              bus_remove_file(@bus, @bus_attr)

            ! the sysfs files usually is created under path
                /sys/bus/<bus-name>/
              e.g.
                /sys/bus/pci/drivers_autoprobe

        Devices
          every device on the system has a device descriptor associated with it.

          struct device
            the descriptor represents a device on the system -- lowest level.
            this structure usually is embedded into the actual device
            descriptor for that certain subsystem,because except the information
            in this device structure,it may needs to track additional
            informations -- like the kobject structure is embedded into others.
            /* such as gendisk,hd_struct,pci_dev, etc. */

          some members
            ->parent
              - points to the parent device -- the device to which it is
                attached.
                usually is a bus or host controller.
                the top-level device has NULL parent.

            ->kobj
              - kobject for this device.
                /* as general rule,this->kobj->parent is equal to
                 * &this->parent->kobj
                 */

            ! 2.6.34.1,member @bus_id is no longer exists.

            ->init_name
              - initial name of the device.
                /* kernel routine device_add() would use this name to set
                 * device's kobject's name,and reset this member to NULL.
                 */
    
            ->bus_type
              - points to the bus this device connected.

            ->driver
              - points to an object is type of struct device_driver.
                the driver manages this device.

            ->platform_data
              - void pointer to platform specific data.
                this data may needed by driver code.

            ->release
              - method to release this device.
                this function will be called by kobject's cleanup routine.

          registeration/unregisteration
            device_register()
            device_unregister()

            /* the kobject of this device would be created under its
             * parent's kobject,usually is the bus's kobject
             * directory.
             * e.g. /sys/devices/isa
             */

          get/put
            get_device()
            put_device()

          device attributes
            the structure device_attributes is no longer contained in
            device structure in 2.6.34.1,it been moved into the structure
            class,and device descriptor has a member named @class points
            to the class this device belongs to.

            <linux/device.h>
              /**
               * device_attribute - device's attribute structure
               * # a device's attributes are collected in the array
               *   this_device->class->dev_attrs,the last element's
               *   ->attr->name must be a NULL pointer.
               * # bus_type->dev_attrs is the default device attributes
               *   for every device added to the bus
               */
              struct device_attribute {
                      struct attribute attr;
                      ssize_t (*show)(struct device *dev,
                                      struct device_attribute *attr,
                                      char *buf);
                      ssize_t (*store)(struct device *dev,
                                       struct device_attribute *attr,
                                       const char *buf, size_t count);
              };

              /* macro used to declare and init a local device attribute */
              #define DEVICE_ATTR(_name, _mode, _show, _store) \
              struct device_attribute dev_attr_##_name = \
                      __ATTR(_name, _mode, _show, _store)

            add sysfs file
              device_create_file()

            remove sysfs file
              device_remove_file()

        Device Drivers
          device model tracks all of the drivers known to the system.
          the main reason is
            to enable the driver core to match up drivers with new devices.

          struct device_driver
            the structure used to represent a device driver.
            /**
             * usually,we mentioned driver is easily to associate it with
             * the actual code of driver program.
             * but for kernel,its actual code is not different to the kernel
             * code,but we need a special thing to identifies these resources
             * that driver program provides.
             * so it is the device_driver descriptor.
             */

            some members and methods
              ->name
                - points to the name of this driver

              ->bus
                - points to the bus_type on which this driver registered

              ->mod_name
                - points to the name of module provides this driver

              ->groups
                - points to an array of pointers of struct attribute_group
                  /* const qualified
                   * last element is NULL as end
                   */

              ->probe
                - method called by bus for driver probe,when a new device is
                  registered,the drivers on bus should be probed to determine
                  whether it can handles this device

              ->remove
                - method used to remove device,hotplug or unload

              ->shutdown
                - method used to shut device down,power state changed

          driver attribute
            in 2.6.34.1,driver descriptor is no longer contains the structure
            driver_attribute,instead,the bus contains the default driver
            attributes,and a driver also can joins to the attribute group.

            struct driver_attribute {
                    struct attribute attr;
                    ssize_t (*show)(struct device_driver *driver, char *buf);
                    ssize_t (*store)(struct device_driver *driver, const char *buf,
                                     size_t count);
            };

            /* macro to declare and initialize a local driver_attribute object */
            #define DRIVER_ATTR(_name, _mode, _show, _store)

          registeration/unregisteration
            /* register driver on specified bus ->bus */
            driver_register()

            /* unregister driver from specified bus ->bus */
            driver_unregister()

          /**
           * creation of driver's sysfs files will be done by bus core,
           * the routine bus_add_driver() would iterates its ->drv_attrs,
           * and for each attribute invoke driver_create_file() on it.
           * # default driver attributes.
           *   if our driver has more attributes,we can invoke this
           *   routine in our module code.
           */
          add sysfs files
            driver_create_file()

          remove sysfs_files
            driver_remove_file()

          ! similar to device descriptor,the device_driver descriptor usually
            is embedded into the actual driver descriptor for particular device.
            such as pci_driver structure,it is the driver descriptor for PCI
            bus.

          ! the owner of driver's attributes usually is the module provides
            this driver.
            /**
             * the container of device_driver does not need to holds a pointer
             * to struct module,we can use device_driver->module directly.
             */
            e.g.
              /sys/bus/pci/nvme
                           |
                           +--> 0000:04:00.0    /* symlink to bus */
                           |
                           +--> bind
                           |
                           +--> module          /* symlink to module -- nvme */
                           |
                           +--> new_id          /* manually bind new device */
                           |
                           +--> remove_id
                           |
                           +--> uevent
                           |
                           +--> unbind

        Class
          a class is a higher-level view of a device that abstracts out low-level
          implementation details.
          class allow userspace to work with devices based on what they do,rather
          than how they are connected or how they work.

          the sysfs directory
            /sys/class

            ! except "block" devices,all devices can be found under
              the directory.such as /sys/class/input,/sys/class/tty,etc.
              "block" devices can be found under /sys/block for historical
              reasons,but "bdi" can be found under /sys/class/bdi .

          class_simple interface
            designed to make it as easy as possible to add new classes to the
            system,usually,is to expose attributes containing device numbers to
            enable the automatic creation of device nodes.

          full class interface
            the "class" descriptor and the accompany components.

          !! IN 2.6.34.1,SIMPLE CLASS INTERFACE BEEN REMOVED.

          class descriptor
            <linux/device.h>
              /**
               * class - device class descriptor
               * @name:  name of this class
               * @owner: module who owns this class
               * @class_attrs:
               *         class_attribute array,the last element's @attr->name
               *         is NULL
               * @dev_attrs:
               *         default device attributes for this class,the last
               *         element's @attr->name is NULL
               * @dev_kobj:
               *         kobject of this class
               *         # if un-specified,__class_register() will set this
               *           member to @sysfs_dev_char_kobj as default behavior
               * @dev_uevent:
               *         method to adds class specified stuff to uevent env,
               *         function dev_uevent() calls to this method if
               *         necessary
               * @devnode:
               *         method called by device_get_devnode() to get
               *         the device's specific name,if @dev->type is
               *         NULL or type->devnode is not implemented,
               *         device_get_devnode() will checks the device's
               *         class and calls to this method if has a class
               *         and implemented this method
               * @class_release:
               *         method to release this class
               * @dev_release:
               *         method may called by device_release() to release
               *         the device,if the device's @type is NULL or
               *         release() is not implemented
               * @suspend:
               *         method called by __device_suspend() as the first step
               *         of device suspend control path
               * @resume:
               *         method called by device_resume() as the last
               *         step of device resume control path
               * @pm:    class specified power management operations,
               *         both methods above is the "legacy class" mode,
               *         if @pm is specified,the power core will prefers to @pm
               *         rather than @suspend and @resume
               * @p:     class private data,points to an object is type of
               *         struct class_private,such structure is defined in
               *         <drivers/base/base.h>
               */
              struct class {
                      const char *name;
                      struct module *owner;
                      struct class_attribute *class_attrs;
                      struct device_attribute *dev_attrs;
                      struct kobject *dev_kobj;

                      int (*dev_uevent)(struct device *dev, struct kobj_uevent_env *env);
                      char *(*devnode)(struct device *dev, mode_t *mode);

                      void (*class_release)(struct class *class);
                      void (*dev_release)(struct device *dev);

                      int (*suspend)(struct device *dev, pm_message_t state);
                      int (*resume)(struct device *dev);

                      const struct dev_pm_ops *pm;

                      struct class_private *p;
              };

          /* top-level class kset */
          <drivers/base/class.c>
            static struct kset *class_kset;
            /* corresponds to /sys/class */

          create class
            /* <linux/device.h> */

            /* calls to __class_create() with a lock_class_key object
             * which has static storage-linkage
             */
            #define class_create(owner, name)

            __class_create()

          destroy class
            class_destroy()

          get/put
            there is no public interfaces to get/put a class descriptor.
            but there is some static functions defined in <drivers/base/class.c>,
            class_get() and class_put(),both method operates on the kset
            ->p->class_subsys(the class_private's member).

          registeration/unregisteration
            class_register()
            class_unregister()

            ! registeration will crease a new dentry under /sys/class,if
              the class is not @block_cls.
              unregisteration will remove the dentry created previously.

            ! the class actually is represented by a kset in sysfs,and
              all classes are registered under the top-level class
              /sys/class -- a kset .

          class attribute
            descriptor is struct class_attribute,defined in <linux/device.h>.
            just a wrapper of struct attribute,like the device_attribute
            wrapper.

            macro CLASS_ATTR(_name, _mode, _show, _store) is used to
            declare and initialize a local class_attribute object.

            simple class attribute
              just a static string,and the descriptor is
                struct class_attribute_string {
                        struct class_attribute attr;
                        char *str;
                };

              function show_class_attr_string() is used to fill the simple
              class attribute string into a buffer.
              /* ssize_t show_class_attr_string(struct class *class,
               *                                struct class_attribute *attr,
               *                                char *buf);
               * # get class_attribute_string from @attr,and copy
               *   the static string.
               */
    
          add sysfs files
            class_create_file()

            ! e.g.
                /sys/class/input <= class
    
                class_create_file() <= class->p->class_subsys.kobj
                create file under /sys/class/input -- kobj->sd

                /sys/class/input/event0 /* usually a symlink */

                # the @sd of the kobject of class's class_subsys that is a
                  kset object,is initialized on the sysfs_create_dir()
                  control path,which is called by a static function create_dir()
                  defined in <lib/kobject.c>,the entrypoint is kset_register().
                  function sysfs_new_dirent() is called in sysfs component
                  to makeup a new sysfs directory dentry.

          remove sysfs files
            class_remove_file()

          class interface
            the interface provides a set special actions should to be done
            when the device_add()/device_del() is called for a device that
            belongs to a particular class.
            actually,device_add() will iterates the class's interfaces that
            device belongs to,and invoke @add_dev() method if it been
            implemented.
            ! then the class the device belongs to can take a place to
              do something under the particular situations.
    
            <linux/device.h>
              /**
               * class_interface - the interface for a device class to manipulate
               *                   devices belong to the class
               * @node:            a list node of the list which collects all
               *                   registered class interfaces to the specified
               *                   class,this member is linked into
               *                   cls->p->class_interfaces
               *                   # where @cls usually is equal to ->class
               * @class:           the class this class_interface belongs to
               * @add_dev:         method adds a device to the class
               * @remove_dev:      method removes a device from the class
               */
              struct class_interface {
                      struct list_head node;
                      struct class *class;

                      int (*add_dev)(struct device *, struct class_interface *);
                      void (*remove_dev)(struct device *, struct class_interface *);
              };

          interface registeration/unregisteration
            class_interface_register()
            class_interface_unregister()

            /* registeration will class_get() the class,
             * unregisteration will class_put() the class.
             */

        The general steps for a device adding
          design device descriptor ->
          device_register() -> device_initialize()
                               device_add() -> /* device_private_init()
                                                * dev_set_name()
                                                * # may called by device_add()
                                                */
                                               setup_parent()
                                               kobject_add()
                                               device_create_file() /* uevent_attr */
                                               device_create_file() /* devt_attr */
                                               device_create_sys_dev_entry()
                                               devtmpfs_create_node()
                                               device_add_class_symlinks()
                                               device_add_attrs(@dev)
                                               bus_add_device() -> device_add_attrs(@bus, @dev)
                                                                   sysfs_create_link() /* bus->p->devices_kset->kobj
                                                                                        * =>
                                                                                        * dev->kobj
                                                                                        * name: dev_name(dev)
                                                                                        */
                                                                   sysfs_create_link() /* dev->kobj
                                                                                        * =>
                                                                                        * dev->bus->p->subsys.kobj
                                                                                        * name: subsystem
                                                                                        */
                                                                   make_deprecated_bus_links()
                                                                   klist_add_tail() /* dev->p->knode_bus
                                                                                     * =>
                                                                                     * tail_of(
                                                                                     *   bus->p->klist_devices
                                                                                     * )
                                                                                     */
                                               dpm_sysfs_add()
                                               device_pm_add()
                                               kobject_uevent() /* KOBJ_ADD */
                                               bus_probe_device() -> device_bind_driver() || bus_for_each_drv()
                                                                                             -> __device_attach()
                                                                                                   |
                                                                                                   |--> if bus->match() returned
                                                                                                   |    TRUE
                                                                                                   V 
                                                                                                   driver_probe_device()
                                                                                                     -> really_probe() -> dev->bus->probe() ||
                                                                                                                          drv->probe()
                                                                                                                          driver_bound()
                                               /* dev->class->p->class_interfaces
                                                *   class_intf->add_dev()
                                                */

          !! THE REAL PROBE IS EXECUTED LATER THAN bus_add_device(),BECAUSE
             THAT FUNCTION JUST ADD THE DEVICE INTO sysfs,ESTABLISHES
             RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN THE BUS AND THE DEVICE IN LINUX DEVICE MODEL.

          /* suppose it is PCI bus */

          pci bus register
            the bus descriptor for pci is "struct pci_bus".
            invoke bus_register() to registers @pci_bus_type with the system,
            in the init function pci_driver_init().

            @pci_bus_type {
                    .name = "pci",
                    .match = pci_bus_match,
                    .uevent = pci_uevent,
                    .probe = pci_device_probe,
                    .remove = pci_device_remove,
                    .shutdown = pci_device_shutdown,
                    .dev_attrs = pci_dev_attrs,
                    .bus_attrs = pci_bus_attrs,
                    .pm = PCI_PM_OPS_PTR,
            };

            @pci_bus_type->bus_attrs {
                    __ATTR(rescan, (S_IWUSR | S_IWGRP), NULL, bus_rescan_store),
                    NULL ATTR,
            };

            @pci_bus_type->dev_attrs {
                    readonly "resource",
                    readonly "vendor",
                    readonly "device",
                    readonly "subsystem_vendor",
                    readonly "subsystem_device",
                    readonly "class",
                    readonly "irq",
                    readonly "local_cpus",
                    readonly "local_cpulist",
                    readonly "modalias",
                    readonly "numa_node",
                    readonly "dma_mask_bits",
                    readonly "consistent_dma_mask_bits",
                    rw "enable" 0600 is_enabled_show(), is_enabled_store(),
                    rw "broken_parity_status" S_IRUGO|S_IWUSR
                       broken_parity_status_show(), broken_parity_status_store(),
                    rw "msi_bus" 0644 msi_bus_show() msi_bus_store(),
                    rw "remove" S_IWUSR|S_IWGRP NULL remove_store(),
                    rw "rescan" S_IWUSR|S_IWGRP NULL dev_rescan_store(),
                    NULL ATTR                
            };

          add a device onto pci bus
            the device descriptor on pci bus is "struct pci_dev".
            routine pci_device_add() is used to add a pci device to pci bus.
              void pci_device_add(struct pci_dev *dev, struct pci_bus *bus);
            this routine does not invoke device_add(),but it does some prepare
            works such as device_initialize() the internal device descriptor,
            and some pci bus specific initializations,then add it to the list
            pci_dev->bus->devices -- the discovered devices list.
            the routine of pci calls to device_add() is pci_bus_add_device(),
            which receives a pci_dev descriptor,and invoke device_add() on
            @dev->dev,create /proc dentry and sysfs dentry for this pci
            device.
            bus's probe method or driver's probe method would be called to
            determines whether there is a driver it can handles this device.

            ! if we want to add a single pci device,the order is
                1> call to pci_device_add() on pci_dev descriptor.
                   /* prepare works */
                2> call to pci_bus_add_device() on pci_dev descriptor.
                   /* really device_add() */
              if we want to add several pci devices,we can invoke
              pci_device_add() several times on the different pci_dev's,
              then call to pci_bus_add_devices(),the function will iterates
              the dicovered devices list,invoke device_add() for each
              pci device on the list that has not been added.

            function pci_device_probe() is called,then helper __pci_device_probe()
            then is called.
              if dev's driver is NULL AND pci_driver implemented probe() method
              then
                pci_match_device() is called try to match the pci_driver with
                the pci_dev .
                if there is a matching -- represented by a pci_device_id object,
                call to pci_call_probe(),thus pci_driver's probe() method is
                called.

            ! pci_device_probe() is called in really_probe() by driver core.

            ! if we want to add a pci device to the bus at higher-level,we can
              invoke device_register() on the internal device descriptor of
              the pci_dev object.
            ! pci_bus_type->match is called by driver_match_device(),and which
              is called on device_attach() control path.
              there is a routine named pci_match_device(),it returns an object
              is type of struct pci_device_id *,which is relied by both
              pci_bus_match() and __pci_device_probe().

          remove a device from pci bus
            function pci_device_remove() will be called on device_del() control
            path -- entrypoint is device_unregister() ==> bus_remove_device()
            ==> device_release_driver() ==> __device_release_driver().
            /* calls to one of bus->remove() or driver->remove() */
            if the device's pci_driver is not NULL,driver's remove() method is
            called,and finally pci_dev_put() is called to decrease the pci_dev
            object's refcount(may executes cleanup).

            ! instead call to device_unregister() on the internal device
              descriptor,we should call to pci_remove_bus_device().
              it will calls to pci_stop_bus_device() and pci_destroy_dev()
              on the pci_dev.
              in pci_stop_dev() -- called by pci_stop_bus_device(),the
              function device_unregister() is called,so we can enter the
              road described above.
              the pci_destroy_dev() can help us to destroy the pci_dev
              descriptor.
                /* it acquires @pci_bus_sem temporarily,delete the dev
                 * from the list of bus collects pci devices.
                 * invoke pci_free_resources() to free resources,
                 * finally invoke pci_dev_put() to decrease the refcount
                 * of this pci_dev.
                 * # pci_dev_put() calls to put_device().
                 */
    
          register a driver on pci bus
            the wrapper pci_driver of struct device_driver is defined in the
            header <linux/pci.h>.

            struct pci_driver {
                    /* used for bus driver list */
                    struct list_head node;

                    /* pci driver name */
                    char *name;

                    /* id table records supported pci devices */
                    const struct pci_device_id *id_table;

                    /* methods */

                    /**
                     * called when a pci_dev is connected to the PCI bus and
                     * the pci core thinks the device can be handled by this
                     * pci driver -- matched,pci_match_device().
                     * return 0 means the driver want to claim this device,or
                     * error code if the driver wont.
                     * # called if bus's probe method is not implemented,
                     *   otherwise,the driver core prefers to bus's probe method,
                     *   in really_probe().
                     */                    
                    int (*probe)(struct pci_dev *dev, const struct pci_device_id *id);

                    /**
                     * called when the pci_dev is bing removed from the system,or
                     * when the PCI driver is being unloaded from the kernel.
                     */
                    void (*remove)(struct pci_dev *dev);

                    /**
                     * called when the pci_dev is being suspended,the suspend
                     * state is passe in @state.
                     * # optional method
                     */
                    int (*suspend)(struct pci_dev *dev, pm_message_t state);
                    int (*suspend_late)(struct pci_dev *dev, pm_message_t state);

                    /**
                     * called when the pci_dev is bing resumed.
                     * # optional method
                     */
                    int (*resume_early)(struct pci_dev *dev);
                    int (*resume)(struct pci_dev *dev);

                    void (*shutdown)(struct pci_dev *dev);

                    struct pci_error_handlers *err_handler;

                    /* Linux Device Model device driver */
                    struct device_driver driver;
    
                    struct pci_dynids dynids;                
            };

            we register the pci driver through pci_register_driver() macro,it
            calls to the helper __pci_reigster_driver().
            the helper will setup pci_driver descriptor,and invoke the
            driver_register() on @driver the internal member of pci_driver
            to register it with the system.
            at the last,__pci_register_driver() calls to pci_create_newid_file()
            and pci_create_removeid_file(),those two function will calls
            driver_create_file() routine if pci_driver->probe is not NULL.

          unregister the driver from pci bus
            corresponds to the pci_register_driver(),pci_unregister_driver()
            can be used to unregister a pci driver from pci bus.
            that function will removes the removeid file and newid file,then
            invoke to driver_unregister().
            on the driver_unregister() control path,bus_remove_driver()
            will be called,in which function driver_detach() is called on
            the device_driver descriptor.
            in driver_detach(),__device_release_driver() is called,and fall
            into bus's remove() method or the driver's remove() method.
            after driver_unregister() returned,pci_free_dynids() is called
            on the pci_driver to free the pci dynamic id resource associated
            with this pci driver.
            /* of course,some sysfs files needed to be remove,too,and
             * some lists needed to be adjusted,etc.
             */

            ! the driver is removed from the list
                drv->p->knode_bus
                /* @p is type of struct driver_private *
                 * the object will be created and initialized
                 * by the bus_add_driver() function.
                 * thus drv->bus->p must been initialized before
                 * call to driver_register().
                 */ 
              the list collects all registered drivers on this bus.

          !! WE CAN KNOW THE PCI BUS BASED ON LINUX DEVICE MODEL,AND
             THROUGH THE LINUX DEVICE MODEL,WE CAN GET INTO THE RIGHT
             CONTROL PATH OF PCI BUS -- THROUGH THE CALLBACKS.
             PCI BUS SUBSYSTEM DOES NOT NEED TO HANDLES sysfs BY ITSELF,
             IT CAN ENTIRELY RELIES ON LINUX DEVICE MODEL COMPONENTS,
             AND THE REAL ACTION WOULD BE TAKEN ON A PCI BUS ATTRIBUTE
             IS DEPENDS ON THE show() AND store() IMPLEMENTATIONS PROVIDED
             BY PCI BUS SUBSYSTEM.

        about hotplug
          the earlier distributions contains a script named "hotplug" placed
          under "/sbin/",it would be executed by kernel when a hotplug event
          happened,in process context.
          now,almost all distributions flied with udevd is installed,and
          the notification is send to userspace through kobject uevent.


        firmware
          firmware is a specific type of software permanently or
          semi-permanently embeded directly into a hardware device's
          memory.
          it usually is a segment of program code embedded in hardware
          device that enables them and their features to function
          properly,it provides low-level control of hardware device.
          for a simply hardware device,FM may perform all control,
          but for a more complex hardware device,FM may provide
          only relatively low-level control as well as hardware
          abstraction to higher-level software such as an
          operating system.

          ! FM is not contained in device driver,such as this form
              static char dev_fm[] = {0xf2, 0x33, 0xb4, ... };
            this will bloats the driver,and hard to upgrade FM,except those,
            license problem may happens.
            drivers containing wired-in FM are unlikely to be accepted
            into the mainline kernel or distributors.

          kernel firmware interface
            the proper solution is get FM from userspace when it is needed,
            download into hardware device in kernel space.
            but we can not open a file contains FM in kernel space.

            <linux/firmware.h>
              /**
               * firmware - firmware image descriptor
               * @size:     size of @data
               * @data:     program code
               */
              struct firmware {
                      size_t size;
                      const u8 *data;
              };

              /**
               * request_firmware - install firmware to device
               * @fw:               a pointer points to a pointer points
               *                    to firmware descriptor,*@fw would be
               *                    set to a memory addres start at where
               *                    the builtin firmware is stored by the
               *                    helper _request_firmware()
               * @name:             name of firmware
               * @device:           device descriptor
               * return:            0 OR error code
               * # EXPORT_SYMBOL
               * # can only be called in user context -- process context
               * # bultin firmware should be placed in section ".builtin_fw",
               *   start address represented by @__start_builtin_fw and
               *   @__end_builtin_rw is the end address -- [) range
               * # *@fw contains a pointer allocated through kzalloc(),in
               *   the memory area,firmware image is stored
               */
              int request_firmware(const struct firmware **fw, const char *name,
                                   struct device *device);

              /**
               * request_firmware_nowait - request firmware in the context where
               *                           sleep is not allowed
               * @module:                  module requesting the firmware
               * @uevent:                  sends uevent to copy the firmware image
               *                           if this flag is non-zero else the
               *                           firmware copy must be done manually
               * @name:                    name of firmware file
               * @device:                  device for which firmware is being loaded
               * @gfp:                     allocation flags
               * @context:                 will be passed over to @cont,and @fw
               *                           may be NULL if firmware request fails
               * @cont:                    function will be called asynchronously
               *                           when the firmware request is over
               * return:                   0 OR error code
               * # EXPORT_SYMBOL
               * # asynchronous version,for user contexts where is not possible to
               *   sleep for long time,cant be called in atomic contexts
               */
              int request_firmware_nowait(struct module *module, int uevent,
                                          const char *name, struct device *device,
                                          gfp_t gfp, void *context,
                                          void (*cont)(const struct firmware *fw, void *context));

              /**
               * release_firmware - release the firmware returned from
               *                    request_firmware*()
               * @fw:               the pointer returned in *@fw of
               *                    request_firmware
               * # EXPORT_SYMBOL
               * # after we download the firmware to hardware device,
               *   should call to this routine to release it
               */
              void release_firmware(const struct firmware *fw);

          work mechanism
            when request_firmware*() is called,a new directory with the device's
            name which is being load firmware would be created under
              /sys/class/firmware
            in the sysfs directory,some attribute files are created
              loading
                userspace process set this attribute to 1 when it is
                loading the firmware.
                after it completed,reset to 0.
                -1 means aborts the firmware loading process.
                /**
                 * loading => 1
                 * write firmware image into data
                 * loading => 0
                 *
                 * loading => 1
                 * write firmware image into data
                 * Oops!
                 * loading => -1 # abort
                 */
              data
                a binary sysfs file used to receives the firmware data,
                userspace process writes the firmware into this file,
                and kernel can receives it through store() method.
                /* the method provided by kernel,not the driver */
              device
                a symlink to the device's sysfs entry under
                  /sys/devices/

            the sysfs file /sys/class/firmware/timeout contains a timeout value,
            if the firmware request is not serviced within @timeout seconds,
            kernel will gives up to the request_firmware(),and returns a
            failure status to driver.

            who write the firmware image data to the "data" attribute ?
            : usually,is the userspace hotplug handler.
              when request_firmware() is called,and builtin firmware has
              no the corresponding firmware image,kernel temporarily
              creates a fw dev,it holds those attributes,and has the same
              kobject name as the device,under /sys/class/firmware/ .
              then invoke kobject_uevent() on the fw dev with KOBJ_ADD,
              and wait the userspace hotplug handler send the image
              through "data" attribute.
              /* on the newer distribution,udevd handles this request.
               * after it detected the uevent,it will tries to find out
               * such firmware image file on the system,and transfers
               * its content to kernel through "data" attribute.
               */

PCI Drivers :            
        PCI Bus
          PCI Bus is Peripheral Component Interconnect Bus,introduced by
          PCISIG(PCI Special Interest Group).
          Conventional parallel PCI runs at 32 or 64 bits and speeds up
          to 133 MB/s.
                  CPU------+ +-------RAM        \
                           | |                   | host address domain
                           | |                   |
                      PCI host bridge           /   \
                            |                        |                     
                            |                        | PCI adress domain
                 -----------+----------------        |
                    |       |         |              |
                  Graphics PCI bus    LAN            |
                  adapter  bridge     adapter        |
                            |                        |
                            |        bus 1           |
                  ----------+----------------        |
                        |                            |
                    SCSI HBA                         |
                        |                            |
                       ...                           |
                                                    /
          the PCI architecture was designed as a replacement for the
          ISA standard,with three main goals
            1> get better performance when transferring data between
               the computer and its peripherals.
            2> as platform independent as possible.
            3> simplify adding and removing peripherals to the system.

          clock of PCI bus runs at 25 to 33 MHz,and 66 MHz and even
          133 MHz implementations have recently been deployed as well.
          it equipped a 32-bit data bus,and a 64-bit extension has been
          included in the specification.
    
          PCI support autodetection of interface boards,and its devices
          are jumperless,configured automatically at boot time.
          device driver must be able to access configuration information
          in the device,in order to complete initialization.
          this happens without the need to perform any probing.

        PCI Addressing
          each PCI peripheral is identified by
            a bus number        # 8 bits
            a device number     # this two      \
            a function number   # total 8 bits  /
          PCI specification permits a single system to host up to 256
          buses.

          ! Linux supports PCI domains,each PCI domain can host up to
            256 buses,and each bus hosts up to 32 devices,and each
            device can be a multifunction board with a maximum of
            eight functions.
            -- 256 * (32 * 8) == 65536
               each function can be identified at hardware level by a
               16-bit address,or key.

          bridge
            a special-purpose PCI peripheral whose task is joining several
            buses.
            for the PC architecture which hosts more than one PCI buses,those
            buses can be connected via the bridge.

          address format
            +--> domain number(16 bits)
            |    +--> bus number(8 bits)
            |    |  +--> device number(5 bits)
            |    |  |  +--> function number(3 bits)
            |    |  |  |
            0000:00:01.0

            for a personal PC,there usually only one PCI domain exists,the
            domain number start from "0000".
            if we ignores the domain number,the address format is seem like
            this
              00:01.0
            right 16 bits.

          three address spaces
            1> memory location
            2> I/O ports
            3> configuration registers

            address-space-1 and address-space-2 are shared by all the
            devices on the same PCI bus.
            address-space-3 exploits "geographical addressing",configuration
            queries address only one slot at a time.

            Geographical Addressing
              this kind of addressing links physical or digital data to
              specific real-world locations.it merges address verification,
              geocoding,and unique location IDs.

            address-space-1 and address-space-2 can be accessed via inb,readb,
            etc.
            address-space-3 can only be accessed via specific kernel functions.
            every PCI slot has 4 interrupt pins,and each device function can use
            one of them,the task routes the pins to CPU is the responsibility
            of the computer platform and is implemented outside of the PCI bus.
            -- PCI specification requires interrupt lines to be shareable.

            I/O space in PCI bus uses a 32-bit address bus,leading to 4GB of
            I/O ports.
            the memory space can be accessed with either 32-bit or 64-bit
            addresses.

            ! addresses are supposed to be unique to one device,the same addresses
              to two devices will making it impossible to access either one.
              because auto configuration,if driver does not touch some special
              registers,this problem would not happens.

            memory and I/O address region offered by the interface board can be
            remapped by means of configuration transactions.
            hence,firmware can initializes PCI hardware at system boot,mapping each
            region to a different address to avoid collisions.the mapping information
            is able to be acquired through configuration space.

            configuration space consists of 256 bytes for each device function,
            /* PCI Express devices have 4 kB of configuration space for
             * each function.
             */
            "layout of configuration registers" is _standardized_.
            unique function ID is holds in 4 bytes in the configuration space.
            driver can identify its device by looking for the function ID in the
            configuration space.
                    +--> configuration space
                    |
                    [ ...| device function K | device function L | ... ]
                         |<----------------->|
                           |
                           +--> [=========================] <= 256 bytes
                                          |----|
                                           |
                                           +--> unique function ID

            ! unique function ID can be found in <linux/pci_ids.h>,or in
              pci.ids of package pciutils.
              # /usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids

            ! device board is geographically addressed to retrieve its
              configuration registers.

        Boot Time
          EC -> Power On -> PCI Device power up -> PCI-aware firmware initialize
          -> firmware performs configuration transactions

          configuration transactions performed with every PCI peripheral,by the
          firmware itself,or by the kernel.for each PCI peripheral,allocate a
          safe place for each address region it offers,the memory and I/O regions
          are mapped into the processor's address space.
          /* drivers now can access to the PCI device,and is able to adjust
           * the default mapping,but it never needs to do that.
           */

          binary file /sys/bus/pci/devices/*/config contains raw PCI config
          information that readed from the device.
          /* file /proc/bus/pci/<bus-number>/<device-number>.<function-number>
           * also contains the config information.
           */

        Configuration Registers and Initialization
          all PCI devices feature at least a 256 bytes address space.    
          the first 64-bytes are standardized,the rest are device dependent.

          /*        first 64-bytes        */

          [0x00, 0x01]        Vendor ID
          [0x02, 0x03]        Device ID
          [0x04, 0x05]        Command Register
          [0x06, 0x07]        Status Register
          [0x08]              Revision ID
          [0x09, 0x0b]        Class Code
          [0x0c]              Cache Line
          [0x0d]              Latency Timer
          [0x0e]              Header Type
          [0x0f]              BIST

          [0x10, 0x13]        Base Address 0
          [0x14, 0x17]        Base Address 1
          [0x18, 0x1b]        Base Address 2
          [0x1c, 0x1f]        Base Address 3
          [0x20, 0x23]        Base Address 4
          [0x24, 0x27]        Base Address 5
          [0x28, 0x2b]        CardBus CIS pointer
          [0x2c, 0x2d]        Subsystem Vendor ID
          [0x2e, 0x2f]        Subsystem Device ID

          [0x30, 0x33]        Expansion ROM Base Address
          [0x34, 0x3b]        Reversed
          [0x3c]              IRQ Line
          [0x3d]              IRQ Pin
          [0x3e]              Min_Gnt
          [0x3f]              Max_Lat

          Vendor ID
          - 16-bit register identifies a hardware manufacturer.
            READONLY

          Device ID
          - 16-bit register,value selected by manufacturer,usually
            paired with Vendor ID to make a unique 32-bit identifier(signature)
            for a hardware device.
            READONLY

          Class Code
          - 24-bit register,top-8-bits identify the "base class"(or group),
            middle-8-bits identify "subclass",the low-8-bits identify
            "programming interface".
            e.g.
              02 00 00 => Network Controller Group
                            Ethernet Controller
            READONLY

          Subsystem Vendor ID
          Subsystem Device ID
          - these two fields can be used for further identification of a device.
            if the chip is a generic interface chip to a local(onboard) bus,it 
            is often used in several completely different roles,and the driver
            must identify the actual device it is talking with.

          The required registers are
            Vendor ID, Device ID, Command Register, Status Register,
            Revision ID, Header Type

            ! every PCI device must contain meaningful values in the
              required registers.

          The optional registers are
            except required registers and Reversed region,the remains
            are optional.

            ! the contents of optional registers depend on the actual
              capabilities of the peripheral.
              the optional fields are not used unless the contents
              of the required fields indicate that they are valid.

          !! PCI REGISTERS ARE ALWAYS LITTLE-ENDIAN.
             ! routines in <asm/byteorder.h> are useful to byteorder
               conversion.

        pci_device_id
          this structure is used to define a list of the different types
          of PCI devices that a driver supports.

          <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
    
            #define PCI_NAY_ID (~0)

            typedef unsigned long kernel_ulong_t;
    
            /**
             * pci_device_id - PCI device identifier
             * @vendor:        Vendor ID
             * @device:        Device ID
             * @subvendor:     Subsystem Vendor ID
             * @subdevice:     Subsystem Device ID
             * @class:         Class Code -- [class, subclass, prog-if] triple
             *                 for this device
             * @class_mask:    Class Code mask for this device
             *                 # @class and @class_mask allows the driver to
             *                   specify that it supports a type of PCI class
             *                   device
             * @driver_data:   data private to the driver
             * # driver can make a list of identifiers to describes what types
             *   of PCI devices it supports
             * # if driver can handles any vendor or device ID,@vendor and
             *   @device should set to PCI_ANY_ID.
             *   @subvendor and @subdevice as the same without loss of
             *   generality.
             */            
            struct pci_device_id {
                    __u32 vendor, device;
                    __u32 subvendor, subdevice;
                    __u32 class, class_mask;
                    kernel_ulong_t driver_data;
            };

          <linux/pci.h>
            /* initialization helper */
    
            #define PCI_DEVICE(vend, dev) \
                    .vendor = (vend), .device = (dev), \
                    .subvendor = PCI_ANY_ID, .subdevice = PCI_ANY_ID

            #define PCI_DEVICE_CLASS(dev_class, dev_class_mask) \
                    .class = (dev_class), .class_mask = (dev_class_maks), \
                    .vendor = PCI_ANY_ID, .device = PCI_ANY_ID, \
                    .subvendor = PCI_ANY_ID, .subdevice = PCI_ANY_ID

            /**
             * e.g.
             *   const struct pci_device_id pci_dev_idtable[] = {
             *           {
             *                   PCI_DEVICE(the_vendor, the_device),
             *                   .driver_data = &local_dev_info,
             *           },
             *           {
             *                   PCI_DEVICE_CLASS(the_class, the_class_mask),
             *                   .driver_data = 0,
             *           },
             *           /* ... */
             *           { } /* zero-filled end,equal to the initializer {0} */
             *   };
             *
             *   this array make up a list of devices ids that supported by
             *   the driver,and it is also used to tell userspace which device
             *   this specific driver supports.
             */

        macro MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(@gtype, @name)
          this macro is used to export device ids to userspace,when new device
          is connected,hotplug helper can load the correct driver module for
          the device through those informations.

          <linux/module.h>
            #define MODULE_GENERIC_TABLE(gtype, name) \
            extern const struct gtype##_id __mod_##gtype##_table \
            __attribute__((unused, alias(__stringify(name))))
            /**
             * unused: compiler should not raise a warning because
             *         it may not used
             * alias:  make an alias for the symbol
             */

            #define MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(type, name) \
                    MODULE_GENERIC_TABLE(type##_device, name)

          e.g.
            MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(pci, wdtpci_pci_tbl);
            =>
            MODULE_GENERIC_TABLE(pci_device, wdtpci_pci_tbl);
            =>
            extern const struct pci_device_id __mod_pci_device_table
            __attribute__((unused, alias(__stringify(wdtpci_pci_tbl))));

          at kernel building phase,depmod will searches all modules for
          the symbol "__mod_pci_device_table",if found,it pulls the data
          out of the module and adds it to the file "modules.*map",then
          hotplug helper can retrieves the informations.

          ! __stringify() is a macro defined in kernel,used to convert
            the symbol to a string.
            <linux/stringify.h>
              #define __stringify_1(x...) #x
              #define __stringify(x...) __stringify_1(x)

          ! MODULE_GENERIC_TABLE() been removed in newer kernel,and
            MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE() has been refined.
              /* 6.19.6 */
    
              #define MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(type, name) \
              static typeof(name) __mod_device_table(type, name) \
              __attribute__((unused, alias(__stringify(name))))

              #define __mod_device_table(type, name) \
                      __PASTE(__mod_device_table__, \
                      __PASTE(kmod_, \
                      __PASTE(__KBUILD_MODNAME, \
                      __PASTE(__, \
                      __PASTE(type, \
                      __PASTE(__, name))))))

              e.g.
                MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(pci, wdtpci_pci_tbl);
                =>
                static struct pci_device_id *__mod_device_table(pci, wdtpci_pci_tbl)
                __attribute__((unused, alias("wdtpci_pci_tbl")))
                =>
                static struct pci_device_id *__mod_device_table__kmod_KBUILD_MODNAME__pci__wdtpci_pci_tbl;

                # KBUILD_MODNAME can be determined on the module's building phase.

        Registering a PCI driver
          pci_driver strcuture
            the structure must be created by the driver program,and used to
            identify this pci driver program.
            # -- section: "register a driver on pci bus"

            the minimal initialization to pci_driver
              @name, @id_table, @probe, @remove

          registeration/unregisteration
            /* impl: <drivers/pci/pci-driver.c>
             * return 0 if succeed,otherwise returns error code.
             * # relies on kernel driver core.
             */
            pci_register_driver()
            pci_unregister_driver()

        Old-Style PCI Probing
          pci driver walk-through the list of all PCI devices in the system,
          to find out a specific PCI device.
          but the related ability been removed,because that may crash kernel
          if pci driver modify the PCI devices lists while a device was being
          removed at the same time.
          in 2.6.34.1,pci_get_device() is existing for search a PCI device
          by vendor/device id.

          <drivers/pci/search.c>
            /**
             * pci_get_device - pci device searching
             * @vendor:         vendor id
             * @device:         device id
             * @from:           where to start searching,
             *                  NULL means start a new searching,
             *                  otherwise,it should be the previous
             *                  pci_dev descriptor returned by
             *                  this function
             *                  # we can set @from to iterate
             *                    each pci_dev has the same signature
             * return:          pci_dev descriptor OR NULL
             * !! CANT CALLED FROM INTERRUPT CONTEXT
             * # just a wrapper call to pci_get_subsys(),
             *   subvendor id and subdevice id are PCI_ANY_ID.
             * # pci_get_subsys() will allocates an object si type of
             *   struct pci_device_id,use the parameters to fill it,
             *   and pass it to pci_get_dev_by_id().
             *   function pci_get_dev_by_id() relies on routine
             *   bus_find_device().
             *   !! pci_get_subsys() CANT CALLED FROM INTERRUPT CONTEXT.
             * # bus_find_device() is defined in <drivers/base/bus.c>,
             *   it receives bus type,device descriptor, void pointer to data,
             *   and a callback named "match".
             *   it will initialize a klist_iter object from the bus type's
             *   klist_devices,and use a loop to iterates each device,
             *   call @match on the device with the data,get_device()
             *   on the device if @match returned TRUE.
             *   ! the @match passed by pci_get_dev_by_id() is
             *       match_pci_dev_by_id()
             *     it just converts the device descriptor to pci_dev,
             *     data to pci_device_id pointer,calls to helper
             *     pci_match_one_device(),the helper will returns
             *     a pointer to the pci_device_id if matched,or
             *     NULL if there is no such pci device.
             *   ! pci_dev has no additional refcounter,thus it share the
             *     kobject's kref of device descriptor.
             *     after driver no longer needs the found pci_dev,must
             *     call pci_dev_put() on it to decrease refcount.
             */
            struct pci_dev *pci_get_device(unsigned int vendor,
                                           unsigned int device,
                                           struct pci_dev *from);

            /**
             * pci_get_slot - locate PCI device for a given PCI slot
             * @bus:          PCI bus to search
             * @devfn:        encodes number of PCI slot in which the
             *                desired PCI device resides and the logical
             *                device number within that slot in case of
             *                multi-function devices
             *                # PCI interface treats multi-function devices
             *                  as independent devices,that is for a function
             *                  of the main PCI device,it is considered as an
             *                  independent PCI device,use slot/function
             *                  to address them on the main PCI device
             *                # slot/function number -- 8 bits
             *                  macro PCI_DEVFN(slot, func) is defined
             *                  in <linux/pci.h>,and is expanded to
             *                    (((slot) & 0x1f) << 3) | ((func) & 0x07)
             *                  7:3 <= slot, 2:0 <= function
             *                  ** bit7 -- bit3
             *                     bit2 -- bit0
             * return:        pci_dev descriptor OR NULL
             * !! CANT CALLED FROM INTERRUPT CONTEXT.
             * # this function will down_read() @pci_bus_sem semaphore,
             *   and release it later.
             *   iterates list @bus->devices,for each element,call
             *   pci_dev_b() macro to convert the list node to the 
             *   container pci_dev(member is named @bus_list in pci_dev),
             *   then test if this pci_dev's devfn is equal to @devfn.
             *   if found,will increase this pci_dev's refcount.
             */
            struct pci_dev *pci_get_slot(struct pci_bus *bus, unsigned int devfn);
    
        Enabling the PCI Device
          PCI driver needs to invoke pci_enable_device() function in its probe
          method,in order to access to this PCI device's I/O region or
          interrupt later.

          <drivers/pci/pci.c>
            /**
             * pci_enable_device - initialize device before it is used by a
             *                     driver
             * @dev:               pci device descriptor
             * return:             0 OR error code
             * # ask low-level code to enable I/O and memory,wake up device
             *   if it was suspended
             * # just a wrapper of __pci_enable_device_flags(),the flags
             *   is IORESOURCE_MEM | IORESOURCE_IO -- we want I/O Memory
             *   and I/O Port
             *   ** may assign IRQ Line for this device,the line number
             *      will be stored into @dev->irq.
             */
            int pci_enable_device(struct pci_dev *dev);

            /**
             * __pci_enable_device_flags - helper that enable pci device with
             *                             specified resource flags
             * @dev:                       pci device descriptor
             * @flags:                     resource flags for behavior control
             * return:                     0 OR error code
             * # the main work is handed to do_pci_enable_device() function
             * # this function first tests @dev->enable_cnt member,it adds 1
             *   to the member atomically,if the result greater than 1,that
             *   means it been enabled,just return 0 to caller.
             *   make use of a for loop,iterates the device's @resource array,
             *   range is idx 0 to DEVICE_COUNT_RESOURCE,this macro is defined
             *   in <linux/pci.h>,as the end of PCI resource type enumerators.
             *   if there is an element in the array whose @flags member has
             *   enabled @flags,then record the index in a local @bar variable,
             *   it is a bitmask type of int.
             *   after loop stopped,invoke do_pci_enable_device(),pass it
             *   @dev and @bar -- bitmask.
             *   if any error happend,we must decrease @enable_cnt by 1.
             */
            static int __pci_enable_device_flags(struct pci_dev *dev,
                                                 resource_size_t flags);

            /**
             * do_pci_enable_device - really do pci device enable
             * @dev:                  pci device descriptor
             * @bars:                 PCI resource type bitmask
             * return:                0 OR error code
             * # first,invoke pci_set_power_state() to set @dev's
             *   power state to PCI_D0 -- fully-on.
             *   next,invoke pcibios_enable_device(),pass it the
             *   descriptor and bitmask.
             *   finally invoke pci_fixup_device(),the request is
             *   @pci_fixup_enable,pci_do_fixups() will invokes
             *   the corresponding pci fixup firmware hooks.this requires
             *   the objects of type struct pci_fixup in given range,
             *   have the same vendor id and device id,or the fixup
             *   can be applied to every PCI device.
             *   ** any one step failed,return to caller with error code
             *      immediately.
             *
             * # on pci_set_power_state() control path,because power state
             *   is D0,pci_platform_power_transition() will be called.
             *   if platform's power manager can manages this pci device,
             *   platform_pci_set_power_state() is called;otherwise,
             *   return -ENODEV,but if @dev->pm_cap is NULL -- has no
             *   Power Management Capability,need to set member @current_state
             *   to PCI_D0.
             *
             * # pcibios_enable_device() is an architecture-dependent function,
             *   on x86,it is defined in <arch/x86/pci/common.c>.
             *   this function first try to enable the required resources,
             *   if succeed,next,test whether this device's MSI is not enabled,
             *   if it is the case,invoke pcibios_enable_irq() to enable
             *   PCI device interrupt.
             *   ! MSI => Message Signaled Interrupt
             *     for this kind of interrupt,peripheral will sends an interrupt
             *     to CPU by write data to a special memory address,rather than
             *     using traditional physical wire pins.
             */
            static int do_pci_enable_device(struct pci_dev *dev, int bars);

        Accessing the Configuration Space
          to access configuration space,CPU must write and read registers in the
          PCI controller,but the exact implementation is vendor dependent.
          linux provides some common routines to do this.
          /**
           * when driver needs to access to configuration space of PCI device,
           * that involved with 8-bit,16-bit,or 32-bit data transfers.
           */

          <linux/pci.h>
            /* wrappers for pci_bus_read_config_*() functions,
             * read data from @where in configuration space into @val
             */
            static inline int pci_read_config_byte(struct pci_dev *dev, int where,
                                                   u8 *val);
                                          /* _word u16
                                           * _dword u32
                                           * # word and dword version.
                                           */

            /* wrappers for pci_bus_write_config_*() functions,
             * write data from @val into @where in configuration space
             */
            static inline int pci_write_config_byte(struct pci_dev *dev, int where,
                                                    u8 val);
                                           /* _word u16
                                            * _dword u32
                                            * # word and dword version
                                            */

            ! pci_bus_read/write_*() functions receives four parameters
                @p1 => dev->bus : struct pci_bus *
                @p2 => dev->devfn : unsigned int
                @p3 => where
                @p4 => data / data location

              in header <linux/pci_regs.h>,some constant macros are defined,
              used to represent registers' positions in configuration space.
              /* the first-64 bytes */
              e.g. PCI_VENDOR_ID => 0x00, PCI_DEVICE_ID => 0x02

        Accessing the I/O and Memory Spaces
          a PCI device implements up to six I/O address regions,each region
          consists of either memory or I/O locations,and most devices implement
          their I/O registers in memory regions,these registers should not be
          cached by CPU.
          PCI device can sets "memory-is-prefetchable" bit in its configuration
          register,that means the I/O register implemented as a memory region
          can be cached by CPU,then optimization can takes place;but the
          non-prefetchable memory access,has side-effect,just as with I/O Ports.

          ! peripheral maps its control registers to a memory address range
            declares that range as nonprefetchable.

          in the six Base Address registers,we can get the size and current
          location of regions -- I/O address space that is memory-mapped or
          port-mapped.
          /* PCI_BASE_ADDRESS_0 -- PCI_BASE_ADDRESS_5
           * for 32-bit address bus,use one register.
           * for 64-bit address bus,combine two consecutive registers,
           * low bits first,high bits second.
           *
           * PCI device is able to offers both 32-bit regions and 64-bit
           * regions.
           * e.g. [0] [1] [2, 3] [4, 5]
           */

          ! the PCI device's I/O regions have been integrated into the
            generic resource management,we can know the region's location
            and size through the resource descriptors.
              pci_dev->resource[DEVICE_COUNT_RESOURCE]
              /* I/O and memory regions + expansion ROMs */

          <linux/pci.h>
            /**
             * the following enumerators defined the kinds of elements
             * in pci_dev->resource[].
             */
            enum {
                    PCI_STD_RESOURCES,
                    PCI_STD_RESOURCE_END = 5,

                    PCI_ROM_RESOURCE,

            #ifdef CONFIG_PCI_IOV
                    PCI_IOV_RESOURCES,
                    PCI_IOV_RESOURCE_END = PCI_IOV_RESOURCES +
                                           PCI_SRIOV_NUM_BARS - 1,
                    /**
                     * <linux/pci_regs.h> :
                     *   PCI_SRIOV_NUM_BARS => 6
                     */
            #endif

            #define PCI_BRIDGE_RESOURCE_NUM 4

                    PCI_BRIDGE_RESOURCES,
                    PCI_BRIDGE_RESOURCE_END = PCI_BRIDGE_RESOURCES +
                                              PCI_BRIDGE_RESOURCE_NUM - 1,

                    PCI_NUM_RESOURCES,

                    DEVICE_COUNT_RESOURCE,                    
            };

            /* macros to retrieve start address,end address,and region flags */
            #define pci_resource_start(dev, bar) ((dev)->resource[(bar)].start)
            #define pci_resource_end(dev, bar) ((dev)->resource[(bar)].end)
            #define pci_resource_flags(dev, bar) ((dev)->resource[(bar)].flags)

            /* macro to retrieve region length */
            #define pci_resource_len(dev, bar)
            /**
             * start == 0 AND end end == start
             * => len = 0
             * else
             * => end - start + 1 # idx start from 0
             */

          <linux/ioport.h>
            /* I/O port */
            #define IORESOURCE_IO 0x00000100

            /* select one of those two types */

            /* I/O memory */
            #define IORESOURCE_MEM 0x00000200

            /* no side effects */
            #define IORESOURCE_PREFETCH 0x00002000

            /* write protect if set */
            #define IORESOURCE_READONLY 0x00004000

        PCI Interrupts
          by the time linux boots,the computer's firmware has already assigned a
          unique interrupt number to the device,and the driver just needs to use
          it.
          the IRQ Line number is stored in configuration register 60 --
          PCI_INTERRUPT_LINE <= 0x3c in the first 64-byte of configuration space.
          1 byte length,support to as many as 256 interrupt lines,but the actual
          limit depends on the CPU.
          if the PCI device does not support interrupts,the register 0x3d --
          PCI_INTERRUPT_PIN is set to 0,otherwise,is nonzero.
          ! PCI_INTERRUPT_PIN is read-only to device driver.
          /* usually,we design the driver program alongside hardware manual,
           * so we can know the device does support interrupts or not
           * support interrupts at the beginning,check 0x3d register is not
           * necessary.
           */

          PCI connector has 4 intr pins,peripheral boards can use any or
          all of the intr pins.each pin is individually routed to the
          motherboard's interrupt controller,so interrupts can be shared
          without any electrical problems.
          next,interrupt controller responsible for mapping the interrupt
          wires to the processor's hardware,this platform-dependent operation
          is left to the controller in order to achieve platform independence
          in the bus itself.

          ! a PCI connector is a physical slot(PCI slot) on a computer
            motherboard.
            a device board(expansion card) is a circuit board that plugs
            into the physical slot.
              PCI slot :
                PCIe x1
                PCIe x4
                PCIe x8
                PCIe x16

              device board :
                graphics card
                network adapter
                ...
    
          a device board can host up to 8 devices,each device uses a single
          interrupt pin and reports it in its own configuration register -- 0x3d,
          different devices on the same device board can use different
          interrupt pins or share the same one.
    
          the value in PCI_INTERRUPT_LINE is determined by firmware at the
          boot phase,it set the register according to how the interrupt pin
          is routed for its PCI slot -- only firmware knows how the
          motherboard routes the different interrupt pins to the processor.
          
        Hardware Abstractions
          we still use the idiom pointer-to-impl to describes hardware operations
          on a pci device,to achieve hardware abstraction.
          there is a structure named "pci_ops" which describes low-level
          architecture-dependent routines,and a member named "ops" is contained
          in the structure "pci_bus".
          /* pci_dev->bus and pci_dev->subordinate are pointers to pci_bus
           * objects.
           */
          except the operations relevant to configuration space accessing,the
          others are re-mapped to memory or can be accessed by CPU via port I/O.

          <linux/pci.h>
            struct pci_ops {
                    int (*read)(struct pci_bus *bus, unsigned int devfn,
                                int where, int size, u32 *val);
                    int (*read)(struct pci_bus *bus, unsigned int devfn,
                                int where, int size, u32 val);
            };

            /* pci_bus_read_config_*() and pci_bus_write_config_*() are
             * depends on these two methods,and defined in
             * <drivers/pci/access.c>.
             * when call method,the core will acquired @pci_lock and
             * disable local interrupt,release and restore later after
             * method returned.
             */

          ! on x86 platform,an object named @pci_root_ops is defined in
            <pci/common.c>,it is a wrapper for callings to function
            raw_pci_read() and raw_pci_write().
            those raw-methods will invoke to the corresponding method
            in @raw_pci_ops.
            both read and write methods are require a pci domain number
            where the domain this pci bus residing,and a pci bus number
            that identifies this pci bus in the domain,as additional
            parameter-0 and parameter-1,respectively.
            pci domain number can be retrieved through pci_domain_nr(@bus),
            and pci bus number is stored in the pci_bus->number member.
            /* pci_bus object is created at system boot phase,each one
             * corresponds to a detected pci bus.
             */
            # usually redirected to pci_conf1_read() or pci_conf1_write(),
              they are defined in <arch/x86/pci/direct.c>,and both functions
              are invoke outl() to write-out the result computed from
                PCI_CONF1_ADDRESS(@bus, @devfn, @reg)
              to I/O Port 0xCF8,then operates on the I/O Port computed
              from base port 0xCFC adds offset get from @reg.
    
            